window.SSS_CHAPTERS=(window.SSS_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":26,"title":"Chapter 26: Fear *","paragraphs":["Nothing.","At first there was only darkness, thick and absolute, as if every sense had been switched off. He could not tell whether his eyes were open or closed. He did not know where he was, what had happened, or why his body felt so heavy and distant.","A dull throb began to spread through his skull, slow and persistent. His fingers twitched against cold stone. The surface beneath him was slick in places.","Sounds returned next, hazy at first, then sharper. Liquid dripped somewhere nearby, each drop striking the floor with a soft, wet slap. A thick metallic smell coated the back of his throat. His own breathing reached his ears—ragged, uneven, too loud in the silence.","His eyes opened.","Everything was red.","Blood covered the floor in a wide, dark pool that crept slowly toward his boots. The sight alone made him shiver. He kept his head still. Some instinct warned him that turning would only show him something worse.","He tried to push himself away from the spreading blood. His arms would not move. Something bit painfully into his wrists. He pulled harder. The restraints only tightened. Rope. His ankles were bound the same way. No matter how he strained, there was almost no room to shift.","A new sound reached him—wet, rhythmic, unmistakably the sound of someone chewing something soft.","He forced his head to turn. The movement made the world tilt. His vision swam, then cleared enough to lock onto a figure a few paces away.","A woman with long red hair sat among the blood. She was not looking at him. Her attention was fixed on the open skull in her lap. One hand reached inside, fingers disappearing into the wet cavity. She pulled free a pale, glistening mass and brought it to her mouth. She chewed slowly. Blood ran from the corner of her lips in a thin line, dripped from her chin, and fell to the floor in steady drops.","Rael’s gaze slid down to the body she held.","Nathia.","The same Nathia who had stood at his back only minutes earlier, who had spent the last of her mana to save them both. Her face was slack, eyes open and empty. The top of her skull had been cracked open like a shell.","His vision widened against his will. Beyond her, stacked against the far wall, lay bodies. Dozens of them. Some were missing heads. Others had long strips of flesh torn away. Limbs lay at impossible angles. The pile rose higher than a man, a silent monument of ruin.","The smell hit him fully then—blood, open flesh, and something sweeter and rotten underneath. His stomach clenched. He turned his head as far as the ropes allowed and vomited hard onto the stone. His body kept heaving long after nothing remained. He could not wipe his mouth. When he looked back, the scene had not changed. If anything, it looked clearer, sharper, worse.","The red-haired woman paused. She lifted her head and met his eyes.","Something cold crawled up Rael’s spine and settled at the base of his skull. Every instinct screamed at him to run, but his legs were useless. His breath came in short, broken pulls. He could only watch as she smiled, lips and teeth painted red.","She was beautiful. Even now the fact was undeniable. Yet all he felt was pure, animal fear.","She tossed Nathia’s body aside the way someone discards an empty plate. It landed with a heavy, wet sound. Then she stood and walked toward him. Each step was slow, deliberate, strangely loud in the dripping silence. She stopped so close he could smell the blood on her breath.","\"Rael Blackthorn,\" she said. The smile never left her face. \"The star. The future of Solaria. The unparalleled genius.\" She leaned down until her eyes were level with his. \"Tied up and shivering like a little girl.\"","Her fingers traced the line of his cheek, leaving a sticky path. \"Such a beautiful face. Before, I could only dream of touching it.\"","Rael jerked his head away as much as the ropes allowed. \"Why?\"","She tilted her head. \"What do you want to know?\"","\"Why kill so many people, Suzy?\"","A soft laugh. \"Oh. At least you recognise me even in this form. The Solaria genius’s brain is still working.\"","\"Tell me why.\"","She shrugged, the motion almost careless. \"Why not? If I can do it, why not?\"","There was no hesitation in the answer. No flicker of doubt. Rael swallowed hard.","\"Who are you?\"","Her smile faded. Anger rose behind her eyes like a sudden flame. She drove one long nail into the soft skin of his cheek. Pain flared bright and sharp.","\"Was I that insignificant to you?\" she hissed. \"That you don’t even remember me? Was I not worthy enough to be remembered by Rael Blackthorn?\"","He gritted his teeth against the pain. \"How do you know my name?\"","She stopped pressing the nail deeper but did not pull it free. Her voice dropped to a near whisper, almost tender.","\"Who wouldn’t remember the star of Blackthorn?\"","\"How can I forget you...\"","---"]},{"number":27,"title":"Chapter 27: Psychopath","paragraphs":["The red hair girl shifted forward, knees settling on either side of Rael’s hips until she was sitting fully on his lap. Rael could feel her thighs on his hips near his genital. She never took her finger from his cheek. Fresh blood welled around the nail and ran in a thin line down his jaw.","\"Do you know how long I’ve wanted to touch you like this?\" she asked. Her voice was soft, almost dreamy.","Rael tried to turn his head away. She caught his chin with her free hand and held it still, forcing him to look straight into her red bloody eyes.","\"At least tell me who you are,\" he said.","\"Aurelia Crimsonvale.\" The name left her mouth like a caress. \"How could you forget me?\"","For a second the name meant nothing. Then the pieces clicked. Crimsonvale—one of the other Sovereign family, equal in power to the Blackthorn’s. No sovereign family member should be here that’s what he thought before. Every Sovereign families children awakened in the capital. It was like an unwritten rule. Yet here she was, deep in the First Realm, after he had transferred from Bloomingdale. It should have been impossible.","\"Why don’t you remember me, Rael?\" she continued. \"We used to eat together. Stay together. All the time at university. I’ve even seen you naked more times than I can count. Do you know how much I wanted you?\"","Rael’s mind raced. He thought what the fuck he had never seen her in his entire life. He had never shared a meal with her. He had never stayed anywhere with her. The only memory that surfaced was a quiet, overweight girl with the signature Crimsonvale red hair who used to trail behind him in the corridors, always a few steps too close. He had never paid her real attention. She had been background noise. A stalker he had simply chosen not to see.","Her hand slid from his cheek to his throat and began to squeeze.","\"Why don’t you remember me?\" she shouted. The pressure increased. Air became thin and sharp.","All his life he had focused on learning about knowledge and training, but that had never meant he ignored the people around him. How had this girl stayed invisible until now? The red hair. The way she used to watch him. The heir of Crimsonvale—yes. The quiet, heavy girl who followed him between classes.","His voice came out in broken gasps. \"I... I rem... remember...\"","The pressure eased at once. A bright, almost childlike smile returned to her blood-stained face.","\"You remember? You said you remember.\"","\"Yes,\" he managed, throat raw. \"The heir of the Crimsonvale family. Aurelia Crimsonvale.\"","Her smile widened until he could see the red between her teeth. He understood then, fully: she was a psychopath and a stalker, the worst possible combination. Awakening had reshaped her body into something beautiful and dangerous, even though half her face was still hidden under drying blood. The soft, awkward girl was gone. What remained was unpredictable and hungry demon.","\"You know how sad I was when I heard you didn’t get a class?\" she said, settling more comfortably on his lap. \"I thought I would never see you inside the Dream Realm.\" She spoke as if they had been closest friends, her tone shifting without warning from wounded to excited. \"After the month of training I asked Papa to let me have my first awakening in Bloomingdale. I wanted to see you again. I never imagined I would find you inside the realm itself. I was so happy when I first saw you.\"","The smile faded as quickly as it had come.","\"Then you ran off with that brown-haired girl.\" Lila. The name hung unspoken between them. \"I wanted you with me. That’s why I brought the Frost Wolf.\"","There was a twisted loyalty in her words, a horrifying kind of devotion. When his own family had turned their backs, she had crossed realms to reach him. Rael forced himself to stay calm. One wrong word and she would kill him without hesitation. He needed to play this carefully.","\"Thank you, Aurelia,\" he said. \"Thank you for coming to see me.\"","Her eyes widened. \"You’re happy to see me?\"","\"Yes. You’re the only one who remembered me after the awakening.\"","The change was instant and manic. She began bouncing lightly on his lap, laughter spilling out of her.","\"You’re happy to see me! Happy to see me!\"","Her emotions swung like a blade. One moment she was deadly serious; the next she was almost dancing with joy. She moved her lips near his and leaned down and kissed him hard while holding his hair, bloody mouth against his. The taste of iron and something thicker filled his senses. He remembered Nathia’s open skull and nearly gagged, but he held it down. She pulled back only far enough to rest her forehead against his, grinding her hips once in slow, absent movement. Tears welled in her eyes.","\"I’m so glad I came to see you.\"","Rael kept his voice gentle. \"Then untie me. So I can hold you too.\"","The words hung in the air for a single heartbeat.","Her entire body went still. The tears stopped. The smile vanished. When she spoke again her voice was flat and cold.","\"You’re lying to me.\"","She stared down at him, red eyes empty of every trace of the joy that had filled them seconds before.","\"You only said that because you want to get away.\"","---","Special thanks -","[Bjarne_H thanks for the golden ticket]","[killuaslayer & ftraj thanks for the power stones]"]},{"number":28,"title":"Chapter 28: Aurelia Crimsonsvale","paragraphs":["\"You’re lying.\"","Aurelia’s voice was flat. The playful light had left her eyes. Rael stayed quiet for a few seconds, letting the silence settle, then spoke softly.","\"I’m not lying. I’m just scared. Anyone would be. But I meant what I said—you came for me when no one else did.\"","That part was true. As twisted and disgusting as everything else about her was, she had crossed realms for him when his own family had erased his name. Some small, buried part of him recognised it.","Aurelia studied his face. \"Oh. You didn’t lie that time.\"","Somehow she could tell when he was lying. The realisation settled cold in his stomach. He would have to choose every word with care.","She shifted on his lap, closer. \"Rael, Rael, Rael... I love you so much... will you stay with me forever?\"","The question was a trap. Saying no would enrage her. Saying yes would register as a lie. He met her red eyes and chose a narrower path.","\"Aurelia I can’t love you, I don’t like the way you are right now.\"","Her expression turned to a childlike face with tears in her eyes. \"Why not? Am I not good enough am I not beautiful enough for you?\"","\"No. You’re very beautiful.\" He kept his voice even. \"But I don’t want to be with someone who kills so many people. If you stop... maybe I could start to like you too.\"","He watched her carefully. She could hear his heartbeat; she knew when he lied. Everything he had just said was true, and she felt it. The tears drained out of her face, replaced by something almost childlike.","\"Okay,\" she said. \"I’ll change. I’ll change for you so you’ll accept me.\"","Rael only nodded. He did not trust himself to speak.","She continued, lighter now, as if the matter were settled. \"But I will still eat them. Even if someone else does the killing.\"","\"Why eat them at all?\" he asked.","\"Well... I’m not human and they are tasty.\"","He stared at her. \"What?\"","She leaned in until her lips nearly touched his ear, voice dropping to a secretive whisper. \"I’m telling you, but you can’t tell anyone okay. We belong to a different race. The Crimsonvales are ghouls. I’m a ghoul.\"","The words landed like a blow. One of the sovereign families of the human kingdom was not human at all. Rael had studied about ghouls before—rare, dangerous, from distant continents. They did not need human flesh to survive; other meat would do. But human flesh gave them power. Strength. Growth. How had the royal family allowed an entire bloodline of them to sit among the highest seats of the kingdom? They had to know.","Aurelia pulled back just enough to see his face. \"So you won’t love me because I’m a ghoul? Even when I kissed you, you were looking at that girl for permission.\"","Lila’s face flashed through his mind. He forced the image down and changed tactics.","\"You want me with you. Fine. Untie me and I’ll stay. We can clear this realm together. You’re stronger than any of them. I see that now.\"","He offered her a future that still included him alive and useful.","Aurelia went quiet. Then she laughed—once, short and humourless.","\"You’re still trying to manage me. Even now. The genius is thinking three steps ahead while he’s bleeding on the floor. Cute. But I can smell the lie on you.\"","Her hand settled on his throat again, not squeezing, just resting there as a reminder. The playful girl was gone. What remained was sharp and cold. She had let him talk, let him believe he was steering the conversation, while she collected every truth and every falsehood.","\"Don’t think I can’t have you just because you don’t like me,\" she said. \"I can keep your soul with me and use your body however I like.\"","She leaned in until her chest pressed against his and kissed him again, slow and possessive. The taste of iron filled his mouth.","A translucent window appeared in his vision.","[Class detected]","[Class: Soulbinder (S-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","He chose yes.","[Class: Soulbinder] (S-Rank)","A rare summoning class that allows the user to bind the soul of a defeated creature and temporarily inhabit its dead body. The soul retains the creature’s instincts and abilities, but the body continues to decay and will eventually become unusable.","[Skills]","[Soul Bind] (Master)","[Allows the Soulbinder to capture the soul of any human or creature and transfer it into a suitable corpse, granting control over the body while retaining the soul’s memories and abilities. The corpse gradually deteriorates with use.]","[Soul Storage] (Master)","[Creates a vast 10,000 sq. ft. storage space within the Soulbinder’s soul where anything can be stored, including corpses, beasts, weapons, and other objects. Stored items remain in their current condition and can be summoned or retrieved at will.]","Bloodline skill:","[Conceal]","[A skill that allows the user to alter their physical appearance, including face, hair, and other recognizable features, making their true identity difficult to recognize. Can be activated at all times; does not require mana, only concentration.]","Rael understood her threat now what she meant by keeping his soul and body kept separately. He did not wait.","He activated Soul Bind.","A white shape exploded into the chamber. The Frost Dire Wolf—Alex’s soul given form—slammed into Aurelia with bone-crushing force, jaws closing around her shoulder and hurling her off Rael’s body. She hit the far wall hard, blood and surprise flashing across her face as the great white beast stood between them, growling low, red eyes locked on the ghoul who had once controlled it.","---","Special thanks to-","[YzolYZ, Harouna_Billal_3607, killuaslayer for the power stones]"]},{"number":29,"title":"Chapter 29: Goodbye *","paragraphs":["(Author Note: This Chapter contains extremely disturbing scene.)","━━━━━━━━ [SOUL BIND] ━━━━━━━━","Soul: Alex","Race: Human","Target Vessel: Frost Dire Wolf","Tier: Elite","Condition: Deceased","Soul Compatibility: 92%","[Transfer Soul to Vessel?]","[ YES ] [ NO ]","[System Error]","[Soul already exists at the targeted beast.]","[Do you want to transfer the ownership of the beast to yourself?]","[ YES ] [ NO ]","━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━","Rael chose yes.","The red in the wolf’s eyes bled away to pure black. Something locked into place—an old loyalty rewritten in a single breath. The beast knew the voice that called it was not Aurelia’s.","Alex came running and lunged towards Aurelia.","Jaws closed on her shoulder and dragged her backward through blood and torn cloth and he kept running. She screamed, more shocked than hurt at first, staring at the creature that had always obeyed her.","\"What—why?!\"","Rael could hear her voice fading while going distance away from him.","Rael did not wait. He used Fireball flared against the ropes at his wrists. The fibers blackened and snapped. He rolled free and staggered upright, hands raw.","Alex had already pulled her deep into the trees. The sound of their struggle rolled back like distant thunder. Rael turned from it. One thought remained.","He has to find Lila.","He pushed into the mountain of corpses. Bodies shifted under his hands—cold, heavy, wrong. Faces he half-remembered from the first day stared up with empty eyes. The smell coated his tongue. Blood soaked through his clothes until he could no longer tell what was his. He kept turning every girl over, every time praying the face would not be hers. He has already let Nathia die because of him. He blamed himself if he was never in this city Aurelia would have never came here in the first place.","A soft, rhythmic sound cut through the silence.","Clk... clk... clk...","He followed it.","He saw a straight tear hung in the air, edges glowing. Light spilled out in all directions, bending the space around the wound. He knew about it. He has seen this image hundred of times it is an exit gate. The realm itself was trying to throw him out. It wanted him to leave.","A crash came from the forest where Alex still fought. He heard a loud boom and at distance between saw four red tentacles flown up in the sky. The wolf was losing. Rael felt the ownership of the soul straining. He should run. The gate was open. He was near enough to the gate.","He didn’t.","He reached for the Soulbinder class again.","Thousands of dead voices pressed against him at once—cold, blue, resentful.","Kill. Kill him. Cursed one.","The words hissed in overlapping layers, dry as dead leaves, sharp as broken glass. Fingers that were not quite there scraped across his skin and failed, stopped by something he could not see. His own soul burned orange against the tide. He closed his eyes and forced himself to listen past the hate.","Then, faint as a dying ember, another orange spark flickered.","Inside a cave. Half-buried under the dead.","He never noticed the cave before because of all these corpses covering it. He tore the bodies aside and got in.","The air inside was colder, thicker, wet with the smell of old blood and stone. Darkness clung to the walls. Only a thin shaft of light from the gate outside reached the far end. Lila hung there, chains biting into what was left of her. Her head hung forward. For one heartbeat he only saw that she was still breathing.","\"Lila. I’m here.\"","He punched the rock until the chains cracked. She stirred at the sound of his voice.","\"Ra... Rael...\"","\"Yes. It’s me. You’re safe. We’re leaving.\"","She didn’t reply to him at first then said \"I... I can’t do that Rael.\"","He lifted her chin with careful fingers.","The world stopped.","Empty sockets stared back at him—dark, wet, wrong. No eyes were there. Only raw hollows where light should have been.","His hands began to shake.","He slowly looked down.","Below the waist there was nothing. Only ragged ends and dried blood where her legs should have been.","The shaking worsened. His breath came short and thin. All the speed, all the fighting, all the running—and he was still too late.","A weak smile touched what was left of her mouth.","\"Ra... Rael\"","\"I’m happy... I got to hear your voice... before the end.\"","She dragged in a thin breath.","\"I’m glad... she didn’t take my ears.\"","Tears came without warning. One, then another, then a steady fall he could not stop.","\"Rael... are you crying for me?\" Her voice was almost gone. \"Don’t. I’m happy you’re here.\"","\"Lila—\"","\"I love you.\" The words came slow, each one costing her. \"First person I ever loved. And the last I ever will. I wish we knew each other from before.\"","Her chest hitched. Air caught in her throat.","\"I wish we had more time.\"","\"Wait. Please. I’ll find something. I’ll heal you. Don’t go. Don’t leave me alone.\"","She smiled the way she never had before—soft, certain, finished.","\"I’m not leaving. I’ll stay just by your side. Just look for me there.\"","Blood spilled from the corner of her mouth. Her heartbeat stuttered once, then stopped.","Rael pulled her against him, he hugged her dead body tightly against himself. Her body started to stiffen as time passed.","\"No. No, no, wait—please—\"","He pressed his face into her hair and whispered the words she would never hear.","\"I... I love you too.\"","---"]},{"number":30,"title":"Chapter 30: Why?","paragraphs":["Human emotions are like countless threads woven through the soul—joy, sorrow, anger, fear, disgust, surprise, trust, and anticipation. Each one is tangled with the others, bound so tightly that pulling at one makes the rest tremble.","You cannot simply cut away a single emotion and expect the others to remain untouched. Remove joy, and sorrow loses its contrast. Erase fear, and courage becomes meaningless. Strip away anger, and even love may lose the strength to protect what it cherishes.","They are not separate threads, but a single tapestry.","And when one thread is torn beyond repair, the entire weave begins to unravel.","Something inside Rael broke some part of his emotions. Something he had never known existed until the moment it snapped. His fear for Aurelia disappeared. His body stopped shaking due to it.","He still held Lila’s body. The last warmth was leaving her. Her muscles had already begun to stiffen. The only question left in his mind was Why. Why did she have to die? She never even wanted to enter the Dream Realm. She had told him she would never return once she escaped from this place. She was a pure soul. All she had done was help him took care of him when he was going to break. And now she was dead because of him. If he had never came to there city all of them would have been alive. She could go back to her sister and live a peaceful life.","He held her tighter. The tears had stopped sometime earlier. Then the connection with Alex tore apart.","A sharp backlash slammed into his chest, like a needle driven straight into his heart. He felt the disturbance in the souls outside. Aurelia was coming. He had to leave. But he could not leave Lila’s body behind.","He lifted her and walked out of the cave. Blood dripped steadily from the places where her legs should have been.","Outside, the air itself had changed there were dark clouds appeared. The corpses that had lain in heaps were now standing looking at him with empty gazes. They formed a wide circle around him, silent and waiting, like a living wall of the dead. Beyond them the dimensional gate still pulsed with light.","Aurelia walked between the corpses. They parted for her without a sound. She was hurt—he could see it in the way she moved—but her eyes were clear and cold.","\"I left you for a minute and you gone to bring your whore,\" she said.","\"Don’t call her that. Atleast she was better than you.\"","Aurelia laughed. \"What can you do with a corpse anyway? Give her to me. I can at least eat her brains.\"","Rael looked at her. \"I understand what kind of woman you are, Aurelia. You’re a sad, evil and emotionless bitch. Do you ever think about what the ones you kill feel?\"","\"What about them? They’re garbage. They live like cockroaches and so what if they die like cockroaches. Big deal.\"","\"They’re humans. They have emotions. They have dreams. You took that away from them.\"","She tilted her head. \"You talk as if I’m the only evil person in the world. You think Blackthorns are innocent you think the kingdom treats them equally? Tell me, Rael—did you ever think about people like them before you were called classless?\"","\"I at least didn’t kill them.\"","\"But you were indifferent. Don’t you know how people like them are treated in this kingdom? They are just tools to be used for the means for people like us. It’s not a big deal if a few them die. More will just appear.\"","\"Don’t speak for all of humankind, you ghoul.\"","\"That hurts Rael. I only told you my secret because I thought you will accept me.\"","Rael glanced at the standing corpses. \"How can you control their souls? Now I know they don’t just follow the command unless they wants to. They don’t follow you on their own will.\"","Aurelia smiled. \"Nothing much. I simply offered them a new body to live in. A soul doesn’t really have much brains anyway. They are easy to manipulate.\"","She stopped. Her eyes narrowed.","\"How did you use my class in the first place?\" She thought for a second, then her smile widened. \"Wait. My class wasn’t the only one you used. Before that you used the fire mage boy’s skill... then that whore’s skill... and now mine.\" Her voice softened with something like delight. \"You can use any skill, can’t you?\"","Rael said nothing.","\"That confirms it.\" She stepped closer. \"Wow. A class that copies other classes. Do you know how many people will come for you once they learn about this?\"","\"God, I love you more now. You’re so special. Become mine, Rael. No one will ever hurt you again. I’ll protect my things.\"","Rael smiled, but there was no warmth in it.","\"That’s your problem, Aurelia. You think of everyone as things to take or kill. A human is not a thing. Maybe if you had come to me without the killings and the games, we could have been friends. But now I’d rather die than be with you.\"","Aurelia’s expression hardened.","\"Then I have to take you by force.\"","Red tentacles came out and burst from her back. One shot forward like a spear at a very fast speed. Rael slowly put his hand up and casted multiple Fireball, but the flame only licked the surface. The tentacle kept coming.","Lila’s body slipped from his arms and hit the ground. The tentacle coiled around his wrists and yanked him into the air.","---"]},{"number":31,"title":"Chapter 31: Blood and Rain","paragraphs":["The tentacle yanked Rael into the air. His wrists burned where the red flesh tightened. Lila’s body hit the ground below with a soft, final sound.","He did not look down.","Ignite.","Flame erupted against the tentacle at point-blank range. The sudden heat forced the flesh to recoil just enough. One hand tore free. Rael reached for the goblin blade still tucked at his belt, reversed the grip, and drove it straight into the thickest part of the tentacle still holding him.","The blade sank deep. Blackish blood sprayed across his arm. The tentacle convulsed and released him. He dropped hard, rolled, and came up on one knee just as three more tentacles shot toward him from different angles.","Fireball.","The sphere of flame met the first tentacle mid-lash and forced it to twist aside. Ignite followed instantly on the second, coating it in clinging fire that made the flesh hiss and shrink. The third wrapped his left ankle and jerked him off his feet again. He stabbed downward, cutting deep enough to make it loosen, then rolled free.","Aurelia stood twenty paces away, six tentacles already regenerated and swaying behind her like living whips. Her shoulder still bled from Alex’s earlier bite, but the pain only seemed to sharpen her focus. She smiled.","\"Still fighting for a corpse?\"","Rael did not answer. He raised both hands.","Two Fireballs left his palms in quick succession creating a smoke field. He fired another fireballs One forced a tentacle to block; the other struck her side and forced her a step back. Before the smoke cleared he coated a broken arrow shaft in flame—Piercing Arrow mixed with Fireball—and hurled it. The burning tip punched clean through a tentacle and set it alight from the inside. The limb thrashed wildly, spraying burning fluid, until Aurelia severed it herself with a snarl.","New tentacles sprouted almost at once. The cost showed only in the slight tightening of her jaw.","Rain began to fall.","It started as a thin mist, then thickened into cold, heavy drops that turned the blood-soaked ground into slick mud. Visibility dropped. The standing corpses became dark shapes in the downpour.","Rael reached for Soulbinder.","The dead souls still hissed around him—kill, kill him—but beneath their noise he found Aurelia’s own soul, a sharp, hungry red. He felt its exact position even when tentacles came from behind the rain and the corpses. He ducked one that would have taken his head, twisted under another, and let a third slam into the mud where he had been standing a heartbeat earlier.","Small Fireballs bloomed across the ground in a rough circle. Thick black smoke rose, mixing with the rain into a choking fog. Inside it Rael moved low and fast. Tentacles lashed through the smoke, missing by inches. He lured two of them toward a dense cluster of standing corpses, then set the entire pile ablaze with a wide Ignite. The dead bodies became fuel. Flames roared upward. The tentacles tangled in the burning limbs and jerked violently as the fire climbed them. Aurelia hissed and tore them free, but the delay bought him three full seconds.","He used them.","Another flaming Piercing Arrow took a tentacle near its base. A Fireball forced her to shield her face. He closed the distance while she was off-balance and drove the goblin blade into the meat of one regenerating stump.","\"Aaaa... You bastard.\" She screamed—more rage than pain—and three tentacles slammed into him at once.","One struck his ribs. Something cracked. Another wrapped his right forearm and squeezed until the bones ground together. The third lashed across his back, opening a long, shallow cut. He was thrown through the smoke and landed hard among the corpses.","Rain washed blood into his eyes. His right arm hung useless for a moment. The blade was gone, lost in the mud.","Aurelia walked toward him through the fire and rain, new tentacles already forming. She looked almost calm.","\"You’re creative. I’ll give you that. But you’re still just a boy with borrowed tricks.\"","Rael pushed himself up with his left hand. The ground was littered with broken rock and lengths of chain from the cave. He grabbed a jagged piece of stone and a length of heavy chain. His hands were already blistered from the constant fire, skin peeling in places, but he tightened his grip anyway.","A tentacle shot forward. He swung the chain like a whip. The metal wrapped the limb and held long enough for him to smash the rock against the point where it joined her back. Something wet and soft gave under the blow. Aurelia staggered. Two more tentacles came at once. He ducked one and let the other take him in the shoulder, using the force to roll closer. Up close he drove the rock into the same wound again, then again, until she finally kicked him away with enough force to send him sliding through the mud.","He coughed blood. The rain tasted of iron and ash.","More tentacles rose. She was no longer playing. They moved in coordinated pairs—two to pin, two to strike, two to cut off escape. He burned one pair with a desperate Ignite at his own feet, forcing them back with the heat. He threw the last of his strength into a wide Fireball that lit the rain itself for a moment, turning the falling drops into brief orange sparks. In that light he saw her clearly: eyes bright with excitement, mouth curved in something close to joy.","She was enjoying this.","A tentacle caught his left leg and yanked. He hit the ground face-first. Another wrapped his waist and lifted him again. He cast Fireball point-blank into the limb holding him. The explosion tore it apart and dropped him, but the blast also seared his chest and arms. The skin there was raw now, shiny and tight. Casting any more fire would be agony.","He still cast.","Ignite on the nearest tentacle. Fireball on the ground to create one last bank of smoke. He staggered into it, grabbed another length of chain, and waited.","The tentacles hunted through the smoke. One found him. He let it wrap his midsection, then used the chain to lash it tight against a half-burned corpse. When the limb tried to pull free it only dragged the burning body closer. Rael set the whole thing ablaze with the last clean Ignite he could manage. The tentacle screamed without a mouth. Aurelia severed it, but the delay was longer this time. The regeneration was slowing. The focus cost was mounting.","She stopped smiling.","\"Enough.\"","Five tentacles came at once from five directions. Rael burned two. He dodged a third by sensing her soul a fraction early. The fourth took his already damaged right arm and twisted until something tore. The fifth slammed into his stomach hard enough to drive the air from his lungs and throw him backward into the mud.","He landed near Lila’s body. Rain washed over her still face. For a moment the fight seemed far away.","Then the tentacles closed in again.","Rael pushed himself onto his elbows. Blood ran freely from his mouth. His hands were ruined—blistered, cracked, barely able to close. The goblin blade was gone. The chain was gone. He forced himself to his knees.","Aurelia walked closer, tentacles swaying like a crown of living blood. The rain plastered her red hair to her face. She looked down at him the way someone might look at an interesting insect that still refused to die.","\"You should have stayed on the ground,\" she said softly. \"It would have hurt less.\"","Rael spat blood and rain. His mana was running low not much he can do now.","The tentacles moved.","---"]},{"number":32,"title":"Chapter 32: Analyze","paragraphs":["Rael’s arms shook as he held the broken ribs. Every breath sent fresh pain through cracked ribs. The rain had turned the ground into a black slurry of mud, blood, and ash. His vision became blurry because of the blood coming from his forehead. Tentacles hung in the air around him like waiting snakes. He could feel the last of his mana guttering out. The skills that had kept him alive for the last minutes were slipping beyond reach.","He could not win a long fight. Not like this.","Even though he didn’t noticed during his fight he burned many of those corpses. During the chaos he had burned dozens of the standing corpses. Each one had given him souls. The total had climbed without him noticing until the system finally spoke.","[System Updating...]","[Installing New Functions...]","[Requirements Detected.]","[Class Upgrade Available]","Copy (F-Rank) → Analyze (E-Rank)","Souls Required: 1023/1000","[Upgrade Available]","[Would you like to upgrade Copy → Analyze?]","[ YES ] [ NO ]","Rael could only see with his one yes and chose yes without hesitation.","[Confirmed.]","[1,000 Souls consumed.]","[Remaining Souls: 23]","[Class Upgrade Successful.]","Copy (F-Rank) → Analyze (E-Rank)","[New Function Unlocked: Analyze]","A new window forced itself open before he could even test the change.","[Warning!]","[Flamecaller has reached a 100% level of understanding.]","[Class Evolution Available.]","Flamecaller (C-Rank) → Infernomancer (B-Rank)","[Required Evolution Resource]","Souls Required: 5,000","[Not enough souls left.]","[Alternative Resource Detected.]","Classes:","Archer (B-Rank)","Soulbinder (C-Rank)","[These classes can be sacrificed as Evolution Catalysts.]","[Would you like to sacrifice Archer and Soulbinder to evolve Flamecaller into Infernomancer?]","[ YES ] [ NO ]","There was no time to weigh the cost. He chose yes.","[Confirmed.]","[Archer and Soulbinder will be consumed.]","[Class Evolution Initiating...]","Flamecaller → Infernomancer","[Class Evolution Complete.]","[Main Class: Infernomancer (B-Rank)]","Power flooded him. It was not the borrowed strength of copied skills. This was deeper, heavier, hungrier. Heat gathered under his skin as if his blood itself had turned to oil waiting for a spark.","Aurelia’s tentacles struck.","One slammed into his side hard enough to lift him off the ground. Another coiled around his waist and yanked him forward. He did not resist. He let the force carry him straight toward her.","She pulled him close, close enough that he could see the rain running down her face and the bright, almost playful light in her eyes. The rain has took away all the blood which was covering her face before. He registered her face in his mind.","\"Last chance, Rael\" she said softly. \"Become mine. I have done all this for you. Just come with me.\"","He looked at her for a long second, blood dripping from his mouth. Then he shouted.","\"Fuck you, you Psychopath Bitch.\"","The Fireball that left his hand was nothing like the ones before.","It began orange, the familiar color of every flame he had ever called. Then the new class took hold. The sphere darkened, deepening to a molten dark orange, then to a raw, violent red. Heat rolled off it in visible waves. The rain around them flashed to steam. The tentacles nearest the fire blackened and split.","\"Wha... What?\"","Aurelia’s eyes widened. She tried to pull back, but Rael had already seized the tentacle that held him. He locked his ruined hands around the living flesh and drove forward with everything that remained of his strength and the force of the expanding blast.","The explosion tore the air apart.","He twisted at the final instant, using the recoil and her own momentum. Her body left the ground. The red sphere carried her backward in a roaring wave of heat and force. She flew straight toward the open gate.","As the light of the portal swallowed her, her voice cut through the roar.","\"I will come to get you, Rael. I love you. You’re mine...\"","Then she was gone.","The blast threw Rael the opposite direction. He hit the ground hard and rolled through mud and broken corpses until he finally stopped face-down in the rain. Burns covered his chest and arms, the skin raw and already blistering. Several ribs had given way. Deep, ragged wounds from the tentacles leaked steadily into the mud. Vision narrowed to a dark tunnel.","He managed to turn his head.","The gate flickered once, twice, then began to stabilize and seal. The light shrank. The tear in space drew closed with a soft, final sound.","Rain continued to fall.","Rael lay half-conscious in the mud, staring at the place where the portal had been. Lila’s body was somewhere nearby. The standing corpses had collapsed again into lifeless heaps. The forest was quiet except for the steady hiss of rain on ash.","He tried to move. His arms would not answer. Darkness pressed in from the edges of his sight.","He was still alive.","For now.","---"]},{"number":33,"title":"Chapter 33: System 2.0","paragraphs":["A S-Rank class is an Anomaly. That’s a known fact to everyone in the world. They are so powerful that someone with A-Rank class can work hundred times more but can’t even touch a hair on there head. That’s why Aurelia was so powerful. After seeing her class he knew he can’t beat her so all he can do was come up with a plan to push her to the dimensional gate. Even after copying her power he couldn’t even understand one percent of the depth of that class. The difference is clear when he copied Flamecaller he has already have a certain level of knowledge and after using it everyday he became proficient in it.","Aurelia will probably look for him after she gets back to Titan. She probably knows where he live but she can’t find him because he was still stuck in the dream realm. He doesn’t know how much time has passed after that.","He has lost too much blood his ribs were broken, hands burned and legs stopped responding to his commands. He can’t even crawl. His vision slowly started to leave. Everything started looking blurry. He cursed his own weakness. Even with such a strong class which can copy any class he wants he still losing his life like a weakling.","Rael internally smiled The higher you go the deeper you fall for him that is the correct logic. He doesn’t know why people called him genius. All he did was the things that everyone does. Studies, Traning and making Connections. He excelled in all of them and people called him genius as if it came to him by pure luck. They didn’t see his hard work. The nights he woke up just to study, The days were he just swing the sword all day until his skins came off. Everyday was a torture he abused his own body to become best to become unbeatable and people loved him they trusted that he will be the future gem of Solaria.","But then he got classified as classless. He thought atleast he will not lose the people who love him. They will not just stop caring for him just because he doesn’t have a class. But it didn’t took one day for them to kick him out and treat him like he never existed in the first place.","When he got the class he thought now he can stand equal to them again maybe they accept him now. He wanted to go back to being loved by everyone again. If Blackthorns know about his new class they will accept him in one go. He wanted to see reaction of the people who kicked him out.","But now none of them matters. He really found someone who cared for him. Lila. The first day when the wolf attacked them she could have left him and run away herself. She risked her life to save him took care of his broken body for days. She was special to him not because he liked her the way she did to him. But the way she care that she was ready to die for him.","And it all happened because of the Aurelia. But she is not alone to be blamed. He already knew how the outskirts people are treated in kingdom. They don’t even let a outskirts person put step inside the capital city. The day he was kicked out they told him to leave the capital city. Before he was blind to these peoples lives. But seeing how a nobel killed hundreds of them without even feeling a ounce of guilt. Aurelia behaviour shows the mentality of the nobels she is just the product of all there cruelty.","He doesn’t wanted to go back to those peoples. He swore if he can get out of here alive he would never go back to them. He felt disgusted from them from his own family who let this madness exists. He was sure his family would also know that Crimsonsvales are ghouls. The race which takes pleasure in eating humans. But they choose to ignore them let them be eat humans it’s like there life didn’t matter.","He saw Lila’s body next to him and put his burnt hand on her hands and said \"I wish we wouldn’t have rushed to get out of this place I wish I could stayed with you for even one more day. In next life maybe we born in a much more peaceful world.\"","The people in this world believe that a humans have seven lives. So your end is just a start of a new beginning. They wish for a dead person to find a thing they wanted most in next life which they couldn’t have in this one.","I’m dying. I could hear my heartbeat becoming slow. I can’t feel my limbs, my hearing and vision also leaving. The only thing I can feel is my palm on Lila’s hand.","Inside his mind he saw a text:","[Do you want to die?]","[Yes] [No]","What kind of stupid question is that ofcourse I want to live.","[In exchange for a life will you accept the fate of the cursed one?]","[Yes] [No]","He doesn’t know what is going on or even what he is seeing is real or not. What if he is imagining this because he just wanted to live.","[In exchange for a life will you accept the fate of the cursed one?]","[Yes] [No]","Again the same text came to his blackend vision. Rael thought Ok why not. This life is already a curse how much different some fate will make it.","[The user accepted the fate of the cursed one. Save @$_&$#ld to become free from this cursed fate. ]","[System 2.0 Installing....]","[System 2.0 Installed]","[Welcome Rael to the life of misfortune.]","..."]},{"number":34,"title":"Chapter 34: Notifications","paragraphs":["Rael’s consciousness faded.","When he next opened his eyes, even a little, he felt himself falling. He had no idea how many days had passed since the fight with Aurelia, or how long he had remained inside the Dream Realm. Distorted lights flashed around him. Space itself seemed to bend and twist.","How much time has passed? he wondered. Time and space are folding together.","He tried to piece the memories back together. The brutal fight. How close she had come to killing him. Then those strange lines of text that had appeared just as he was about to die—something about a cursed fate.","He turned his head toward the direction of the fall and saw a white light, a hole in the darkness. His body was being pulled straight toward it. He crossed the threshold.","Darkness.","He gasped a long, sharp breath and slowly opened his eyes.","I can see.","He was lying in his own room—the same small apartment he had left behind before being transferred to the Dream Realm. He was alive. He pinched his cheek hard. Pain flared. It was real.","The first thing that filled his vision was a wall of system notifications.","[System Notification]","[Welcome Back, Rael.]","[Congratulations on successfully returning from the First Dream Realm.]","[You have accomplished an achievement that countless Awakeners have failed to accomplish.]","[Impossible Feat Completed.]","[Congratulations!]","[You have obtained the first version of System 2.0.]","[Warning: System 2.0 is currently in its initial development stage.]","[You are the FIRST individual in the world to possess System 2.0.]","[New Function Unlocked: Rewards.]","[From now on, completing certain impossible feats will grant special rewards.]","[Reward Calculation...]","[Impossible Feat: Slaying a High-Level Savage-Tier Beast.]","[Target: Ironhide Gorilla — Slain.]","[Target: Ape — Slain.]","[Reward Granted.]","[All Attributes +20%.]","[Strength +20%]","[Agility +20%]","[Mana +20%]","[Vitality +20%]","[New Main Class Unlocked.]","[Flamecaller has evolved.]","[Class: Infernomancer (B-Rank)]","[New Infernomancer Skills Unlocked.]","[Inferno Bolt] (Expert)","Fires a compressed bolt of blazing infernal fire that pierces defenses and explodes upon impact.","[Hellfire] (Expert)","Summons powerful flames that continuously burn enemies and are difficult to extinguish.","[Flame Manipulation] (Master)","Allows precise control over fire, shaping it into weapons, barriers, blades, and other forms.","[Infernal Domain] (Locked)","Creates an area engulfed in hellfire, greatly increasing the power of the Infernomancer’s fire skills while continuously damaging enemies within it.","[Inferno Burst] (Locked)","Releases a massive explosion of fire around the user, devastating everything within range.","[Infernal Sovereignty] (Locked)","Allows the Infernomancer to completely dominate flames within its domain.","[Unlock Condition: Unknown.]","[Bloodline Skill Detected.]","[Conceal] (Master)","Allows the user to alter their physical appearance, making their identity difficult to recognize.","Mana Cost: None.","[New System Function Unlocked.]","[Skill Mastery System.]","[You can now increase the mastery of your skills through continuous use and understanding.]","[Skill mastery can now be viewed as a percentage in the Status.]","[Higher mastery allows greater control, efficiency, and power.]","[Continue growing, Rael.]","[The path ahead is yours to conquer.]","[System 2.0 Initialization Complete.]","So many notifications appeared at once that he felt overwhelmed. He was still lying on the bed, staring at the new features.","First, a completely new System 2.0—something no one else possessed. Second, he now had permanent skills from his new main class, Infernomancer. It was no longer a temporary copied class. His original Copy class had also upgraded to Analyze (E-Rank). Somehow he had even retained Conceal, Aurelia’s bloodline skill, without any time limit. Third, there was a new reward system that granted bonuses for completing impossible feats. Fourth—and perhaps most important—he could now raise skill mastery through use and understanding alone, without spending souls. They are all unheard of. He checked his Status screen to make sure what he seen just now is real or not.","╔═════════ STATUS ══════════╗","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Level: 13","Main Class: Analyze (E-Rank), Infernomancer (B-Rank)","Copied Class: None","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign","Stage: Dormant","Effect: +100% Growth Multiplier","━━━━━━━━ ATTRIBUTES ━━━━━━━━","Strength: 50","Agility: 58","Mana: 55","Vitality: 50","Unallocated Stats: 6","━━━━━━━━ RESOURCES ━━━━━━━━━","Souls: 23","HP: 42 / 42","━━━━━━━━ SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━","Inferno Bolt (Expert) (5%)","Hellfire (Expert) (3%)","Flame Manipulation (Master) (4%)","━━━━━━ BLOODLINE SKILLS ━━━━━━━","Conceal (Grandmaster)","╚═════════════════════════╝","The status window itself had changed a great deal. It looked cleaner, more detailed, and completely different from before.","He cleared the last of the notifications and finally looked around the room. The ceiling was the same, yet something felt off. His blue blanket had somehow become pink. The place was far too clean. He used to leave empty food boxes and clothes scattered across the floor. None of that was here. He had expected the apartment to reek after so much time without care, but the air was fresh.","Then he felt something was on his chest then he turned his head to the side.","A beautiful naked girl with a black ponytail lay beside him, her hairs were covering her delicate features and her body pressed against his, arms wrapped around him in a quiet hug.","Rael stared.","\"What the fuck.\"","---"]},{"number":35,"title":"Chapter 35: Girl in bed *","paragraphs":["Rael stared at the very beautiful girl still wrapped around him. Then he looked down at himself.","He was completely naked. No shirt. No pants. Nothing. He could feel her body heat against his chest.","Why? Where are my clothes? And what is this woman doing in my bed?","He glanced around carefully, making sure it really was his room. The walls and layout were the same, but the place felt different—brighter, softer, almost girly. The air carried a faint, clean scent instead of the stale smell he had expected. Someone else had clearly been living here while he was gone.","The girl stirred at the sound of him shifting. She opened her eyes slowly. Grey eyes, still hazy with sleep, focused on him in confusion.","Rael’s mouth went dry. She slowly sat up, and the blanket slipped. He could see everything. Her breasts were large, impossible to ignore. He had never seen a naked girl before. His mind simply stopped.","The only word that managed to leave his mouth was:","\"Pink.\"","She squinted at him. \"What are you saying?\"","Rael jerked his head away, face burning. \"Excuse me... you’re not wearing a shirt.\"","The girl looked down at herself then at Rael then again at herself. Consciousness returned all at once. Her eyes widened. She let out a sharp scream and yanked the blanket up to cover her chest.","The sudden pull dragged the blanket completely off Rael’s legs.","Now she could see him.","Rael looked down at himself—already reacting—and then back at her. She couldn’t seem to look away. He had no idea what was going through her head. He quickly covered himself with both hands.","The silence that followed was thick and painful.","He slowly understood the situation, at least partly. He had somehow returned from the Dream Realm to the exact place he had left. He had no idea how long he had been gone. And this girl had clearly bought or moved into his apartment. Any second now she would start screaming for the authorities.","He turned toward her carefully, trying to keep his voice calm.","\"Wait. I can explain.\"","She raised one hand, still clutching the blanket to her chest with the other. \"Wait, wait. I can understand the situation on my own.\"","Rael blinked. \"Really? You can? How do you know?\"","She pulled the blanket tighter and stood up, keeping her body covered as best she could. Her figure was impossible to miss even then—very full in the chest and hips. She grabbed a pillow from the bed and threw it at him.","\"Cover it.\"","He caught the pillow and quickly placed it over himself.","She bowed her head slightly, face already red. \"I’m sorry.\"","Rael stared. \"What? Why are you apologizing?\"","\"I’m sorry... I don’t remember your name.\" Her voice was quiet, thick with embarrassment. \"I forget things after I get drunk. Even after we... did it... I still don’t remember your name.\"","Rael’s thoughts stalled for a second. She thinks we had sex.","He could use that. If he played along, maybe he could get out of this without the authorities getting involved.","\"It’s okay,\" he said carefully. \"I can understand. I’m not hurt.\"","She looked at him, still flushed. \"Thank you for understanding.\"","The tension in the room eased just a little. She glanced around with pink cheeks.","\"I’m going to get dressed. Where are your clothes? I can give them to you.\"","Rael looked around the floor. \"To be honest... I also don’t know where my clothes are.\"","She searched the room for a moment, bending slightly to look under the bed and then Rael could see her ass he turned his head again, then she straightened up again. \"I can’t find them either. Wait—the last guy who lived here left some clothes behind. Maybe I can find those for you.\"","\"Thank you,\" Rael said, trying not to let the irony show. She was about to offer him his own clothes.","She started to turn away to change. He spoke before she could leave.","\"Wait. Can I know your name?\"","She stopped. Her ears turned red. \"I... also didn’t tell you my name.\"","Rael quickly shook his head. \"Wait, wait. Maybe you told me and I forgot.\"","She nodded, clearly choosing to believe the lie because it was less humiliating. \"Sa... Samantha Ross.\"","She looked at him again. \"Could you tell me your name again I promise I will not forget it this time.\"","Rael hesitated only a fraction of a second.","\"Rael Blac— Ashborne. Rael Ashborne.\"","He could not risk giving his real family name. Not until he knew whether she was safe or not.","Samantha nodded once more, still embarrassed, and left the room to find clothes.","Rael sat on the edge of the bed, pillow still covering him, and let out a quiet breath. He was oddly proud of how he had handled it. Instead of ending up with a crime report, he had received apologies.","But something else caught his attention.","Before he awakened, he had never been able to sense much around ordinary people at that time everyone was equal. But now he could feel the density of mana in the air around her. It was thick. Heavy. Controlled.","She was powerful. Very powerful.","Right now, he was nowhere near her level.","---","[Strom2warden thanks for the golden ticket.]"]},{"number":36,"title":"Chapter 36: Getting Dressed","paragraphs":["Samantha stepped into the other room and closed the door behind her. Boxes filled with the previous owner’s belongings sat stacked against the wall. Her face was still burning. Her breathing came too fast.","What did I just do?","She had given her first time to a complete stranger. The image of him earlier flashed through her mind and she pressed a hand to her stomach, cheeks growing even hotter. She recalled something that size... had that really been inside her last night?","\"Argh... Why can’t I remember anything?\"","She let out a quiet, frustrated sound. Why couldn’t she remember anything? It was always the same. She was a light drinker. One too many and entire stretches of the night simply vanished. She tried to piece it together. She remembered going out with her friends. She remembered Susan her best friend trying to stop her from ordering another round. She remembered Susan carrying her in the streets. After that—nothing. No explanation for how a man had ended up in her bed.","She was twenty-six years old plus she was a virgin. She had always wanted her first time to mean something, to be with someone special. Instead she couldn’t even be sure it had happened, and the only person who might know was sitting in the next room. What must he think of her? That she was the kind of girl who brought anyone home?","She cracked the door open just enough to look.","Rael sat at the bed and looked near the window, looking out. For a moment she forgot her panic. He was unfairly handsome. Where did she find such a handsome face last night. At least she hadn’t ended up with someone ugly. God, how could a person look like that?","He turned and their eyes met.","\"Did you find the clothes?\" he asked.","She snapped out of it. \"Yeah—yeah, one minute.\"","She dressed quickly in a pink T-shirt and shorts, her usual home clothes, then fixed her hair in the mirror. The first impression was already a disaster; she refused to make it worse. Inside one of the boxes she found a grey T-shirt, a black jacket, and black pants. She still couldn’t stop wondering what had happened to his original clothes. A new, mortifying thought appeared: had they done it outside? Her face heated all over again.","She took a few deep breaths until the worst of the panic settled, then walked back out. Rael looked at her all dressed and all he could think about is how cute she looked. It was as if he got himself a girlfriend just now. Rael still sat on the edge of the bed with the pillow firmly in place. The sight made the whole situation feel even more uncomfortable, but at least he was covered.","He accepted the clothes with a quiet \"Thank you.\"","\"I’m sorry,\" she said again.","\"It’s okay.\"","She hesitated, then spoke in a rush. \"I’m not that kind of girl.\"","Rael looked up. \"What?\"","\"I was just too drunk. That’s why... those things happened. Please don’t think of me as one of those girls.\"","\"Samantha,\" he said, voice steady, \"I never thought of you like that. You’re clearly a sweet person. Why would I?\"","Some of the tension left her shoulders. \"Do you want coffee?\"","\"Of course.\"","Internally he was already calculating how quickly he could leave. If she ever learned the truth, she might actually kill him. He needed to get out of here soon.","\"Can I change in that room?\" he asked.","\"Sure.\"","He stepped into the side room and closed the door. The moment he was alone he started going through the boxes, searching for his phone. Without it he couldn’t access any of his money, and he still needed to keep his real identity hidden. One box, then another. He carefully put everything back each time. Nothing.","The door opened behind him.","\"What are you doing?\"","Samantha’s voice was suddenly cold. The shy, embarrassed girl from a minute ago was gone. A sharp, heavy presence filled the small space.","Rael turned slowly and picked up a pair of shoes that happened to be in front of him. \"I was looking for shoes.\"","The coldness faded almost at once. \"Oh. I’m sorry. I thought you were... never mind. Did you find them?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"Okay. Coffee’s ready.\"","\"Coming.\"","He finished dressing quickly. As he turned, a faint reflection of light caught his eye. His phone sat in an open cupboard. He slipped it into his pocket without a sound.","When he walked back into the main room, fully dressed, Samantha’s breath caught for half a second before she remembered the two cups in her hands. She set them down. Rael took a seat at the small table, picked up the coffee, and took a quiet sip.","The awkward silence settled between them again.","---"]},{"number":37,"title":"Chapter 37: Conceal","paragraphs":["Rael sat across from Samantha, both hands wrapped around the warm cup. He wanted to finish the coffee quickly and leave, but it was still too hot. Every sip burned a little. The silence between them stretched, thick and uncomfortable. He decided to break it first.","\"So, Samantha... you’re from Bloomingdale?\"","She stopped mid-sip and looked at him. She understood he was trying to ease the tension. A small, polite smile appeared.","\"No. I only moved here last month.\"","\"Oh. Why?\"","\"I’m sorry, I can’t really say. It’s work-related.\"","\"Okay. I understand.\"","She tilted her head slightly. \"What about you, Rael? Did you live in Bloomingdale?\"","He paused, choosing his words carefully. \"No. I’m from Ashfort.\"","Ashfort was a small city on the outskirts. Most people from the capital or larger towns barely knew anything about it. It felt safe enough to mention.","\"I remember that place,\" she said. \"I lived there for a while once. Which part?\"","Rael kept his expression calm. \"Near the river.\"","For a second he thought he had said too much. Her eyes stayed on him a moment longer than necessary, but then she only nodded.","\"Oh, that river. I lived nearby too. Weird that we end up meeting all the way here in Bloomingdale, huh?\"","He managed a small smile. \"Yeah. Weird.\"","The conversation settled into something almost normal. Samantha looked down at her cup, then spoke again, quieter this time.","\"I’m sorry for barging in on you while you were changing.\"","He shook his head. \"It’s fine.\"","She hesitated. \"There’s something else. I don’t know if I should tell you, but... maybe it’s better if you know. For your own safety.\"","Rael set his cup down. \"What is it?\"","She took a breath. \"You know the latest awakening didn’t go like the others.\"","\"What do you mean?\"","\"A lot of people who entered the First Realm never came back. Some of the ones who did escape said they saw high-level monsters in there—things that shouldn’t exist in the beginner stage. The whole city feels like it’s in mourning right now. So many families lost their children on their first awakening. It’s... really sad.\"","Rael already knew the reason. Aurelia. He kept his face neutral.","\"Yeah,\" he said quietly. \"It’s very sad.\"","Samantha continued, her voice lower. \"There’s more. Before the authorities arrived, there was a string of killings in this area. A lot of people died. Once the officials showed up, the murders stopped. Still... it’s better to be careful.\"","She watched his reaction for a second, then waved a hand as if dismissing the heavier topic. \"Anyway. Let’s leave it at that.\"","Rael understood more than she realized. Aurelia had been here looking for him. The thought settled cold in his chest.","He finished what was left of the coffee and stood up. \"I should get going.\"","Samantha looked a little surprised, as if she had only just started feeling comfortable talking. \"Oh... okay. Maybe see you later?\"","\"Yeah. Take care.\"","\"Bye.\"","He stepped out into the hallway and closed the door behind him. The moment it clicked shut, he pulled out his phone. In this world, smartphones charged under sunlight. The moment the light touched the screen, it flickered to life.","A chilly wind moved through the open corridor. He checked the date.","Novar.","He had awakened in the month of Jular. Four months. He had been gone for four entire months.","He typed his own name into the search bar. Article after article appeared.","[WHERE IS RAEL BLACKTHORN?","Rael Blackthorn was a failed awakener and has been missing since the month of Jular. No body has been found, and no one knows his whereabouts.","Dead? Missing? Or still alive?","Authorities have yet to confirm his fate.]","[RAEL BLACKTHORN: DEAD OR MISSING?","The disappearance of Rael Blackthorn has sparked rumors across the city. Some believe he was killed by a powerful beast. Others believe he is still somewhere within the city, hiding in shame. His current status remains unknown.]","[THE MYSTERY OF RAEL BLACKTHORN","Rael Blackthorn hasn’t returned.","His family remains silent, while rumors continue to spread.","Was he killed? Is he trapped? Or is Rael Blackthorn still alive? For now, no one knows.]","He stared at the headlines for a long moment. He could not walk around looking like himself. Samantha might not have recognized him, but plenty of others would.","He opened his status and focused on the bloodline skill.","[Conceal]","A skill that allows the user to alter their physical appearance, including face, hair, and other recognizable features, making true identity difficult to recognize. Can be activated at all times. Requires only concentration. No mana cost.","It was the same skill Aurelia had used to hide as Suzy for so long. He still didn’t know how he still retained this skill. Maybe it is because it’s an Bloodline skill different from normal skill and his class can make Bloodline skill permenently. He smiled and said \"How convenient.\"","Rael concentrated. A faint shift passed over his features— his face started to mold as he wants he kept molding nothing dramatic, just enough to make his face ordinary, forgettable. When he checked his reflection in the dark screen of the phone, the person looking back was no longer Rael Blackthorn.","He slipped the phone into his pocket and started walking toward the lift, the cold wind following him down the empty corridor.","---"]},{"number":38,"title":"Chapter 38: City","paragraphs":["Rael stepped out of the apartment building and into the street. The first thing that hit him was the smell—thick, metallic smoke mixed with the sharp tang of ozone. The outskirts had always had poor air quality, but after months inside the Dream Realm the difference felt almost physical. He drew a slow breath and tasted grit on his tongue.","Bloomingdale stretched out in front of him like a half-finished dream of the future. Before mana stones were discovered, technology had stalled for decades. Energy was the bottleneck that kept every ambitious design on paper. Once high-level monsters started dropping those glowing cores—essentially the crystallized hearts of beasts—everything changed. Mana stones powered the smallest clock battery and the massive arc reactors that kept entire city districts lit. There was almost no limit left to what people could invent. The only real barrier now was how many stones you could afford.","That was why everyone still chased awakening. The gap in wealth between a normal citizen and an awakener was brutal. One successful run through a dungeon could set a family up for life. Rael understood the hunger better than most.","He started walking with his hands in his pockets, mind already sorting priorities. First he needed a new place to stay. The apartment he had bought with the compensation money was gone—sold out from under him while he was trapped in the realm. The thought still made him angry. The owner had no right to flip the place the moment the tenant disappeared. Second, he had to ditch the phone. Even if his family no longer cared enough to track him, someone else might. Transferring the money into fresh crypto-credit accounts would come right after that. And above everything else he needed an official Awakener ID from the Awakener Authority Association—AAA for short. Walking around awakened without one was enough to get labeled a terrorist. The Association kept records on every single person who returned from the realms. There was no way around it.","The city itself was not wealthy, but it was far from a slum. Neon signs in harsh blues and sickly greens flickered along the lower floors of the towers. Holographic advertisements floated at street level, cycling through offers for cheap mana-stone chargers, black-market skill manuals, and \"guaranteed\" gate maps that everyone knew were fake. Above him, layered traffic moved in three distinct bands. Closest to the ground were the ordinary wheeled vehicles—old combustion engines converted to stone power, still the only option most people could afford. Higher up, sleek flying bikes and compact cars slid through designated air lanes, their undersides glowing soft violet. Highest of all ran the elevated metro lines, silver carriages streaking past between the towers with a low magnetic hum.","Tall buildings packed so tightly they stole most of the remaining daylight. What little sky showed between them was the color of dirty steel. Winter had arrived while he was gone. The wind cut through his borrowed jacket and carried the constant low roar of the city—engines, distant sirens, the occasional crackle of a failing neon tube. He had been missing for more than four months. The news articles about Rael Blackthorn were already three months old. The media had moved on. Even the public had started to forget.","From those same articles he had learned that his family never admitted to throwing him out. They had simply let the story settle as \"missing.\" Cleaner that way. No bad publicity, no awkward questions about why the once-promising heir had been discarded the moment he failed to awaken. The elders had almost certainly suggested the approach. Rael felt a familiar irritation rise. One day he would settle that particular debt. Not today.","His fingers brushed the storage ring on his right hand. Lila’s ring. He stopped walking for a moment and focused on it. A translucent panel unfolded in his vision, listing everything still stored inside. The preserved corpses of the horned rabbits and the wild boars they had hunted. A few scavenged weapons. And there, near the bottom, the simple necklace she had pressed into his hand.","If anything happens to me, give this to my sister.","He closed the panel and let his hand fall. \"I’m sorry, Lila,\" he muttered under his breath. \"You’ll have to wait a little longer. I promise I’ll find her.\" The Awakener Association would have records. Once he had an ID he could start looking.","He kept walking. The sidewalks were crowded with people in heavy coats and cheap thermal masks, most of them keeping their heads down against the cold. Street vendors sold skewers of synthetic meat that hissed on mana-heated grills. A pair of enforcement drones drifted past overhead, red scanners sweeping the crowd. No one paid them any attention.","Up ahead a glowing board advertised a mobile store, the letters flickering between white and pale cyan. He needed a new phone. To reach it he would have to cross the wide avenue. Ground traffic still dominated this level—long lines of converted cars and battered delivery trucks waiting at the signal. Flying vehicles hummed above them, ignoring the lights entirely.","Rael stopped at the curb and waited for the signal to turn red. The cold wind tugged at his jacket. Neon reflected in the oily puddles at his feet. Somewhere far above, a metro carriage screamed past between the towers, leaving a brief trail of blue light.","He was back in his world.","---"]},{"number":39,"title":"Chapter 39: New Phone","paragraphs":["A heavy truck rumbled past, kicking up a thin spray of oily water from the street. On the side of its cargo container a large poster glowed under the neon lights. The image showed the members of Eclipse—the guild that had recently cleared an S-rank dungeon the rising stars of Solaria. Each of the members are like some kind of celebrity. At the center of the poster stood Kaia Blackthorn, his elder sister, expression calm and sharp as always.","Rael slowed for a moment. He had once looked up to her more than anyone. She was the first in the family to walk away from the Blackthorn name and build something of her own as co-leader of Eclipse. As a child she had been kind to him they used to be close a proper siblings. Somewhere along the way that warmth had turned cold. She was the one who distanced herself from him. He could guess at the reasons, but he had never been certain. He had accepted her silence years ago, yet the respect had never completely left him.","The traffic signal finally flipped to red. He stepped off the curb and walked quickly across the avenue. A hover bike shot past him so close that the wind from its thrusters tugged at his jacket. The rider ignored the red light completely. Two enforcement drones dropped from a higher lane, red scanners locking onto the bike’s registration number. The fine would be automatic and expensive. Rael didn’t bother watching. He kept moving.","The mobile store sat a short distance ahead. A large green board hung above the entrance, the kind that was supposed to signal an eco-friendly business. In this part of the city the claim was almost always a joke. He pushed the door open. A small bell chimed overhead.","Inside, the shop was narrow and cluttered. Shelves lined both walls, stacked with second-hand freezers, mana-powered ovens, cracked display tablets, and open boxes of phone casings. Neon strips along the ceiling cast a harsh white light that made every surface look slightly dirty. The air smelled of plastic, old circuitry, and the faint ozone of low-grade mana batteries.","Behind the counter an old man sat on a high stool. He was bald, the skin of his head shiny under the lights, and a heavy belly pushed out against a stained work shirt. He had one finger buried in his ear when Rael entered. The moment he noticed a customer, the finger came out and a practiced smile spread across his face.","\"Welcome, sir.\"","Rael walked up to the counter. The old man leaned forward, belly resting against the edge.","\"So what can I get you?\"","Rael glanced at the shelves. \"I’m looking for a phone.\"","The owner’s smile widened. He bent down with a grunt, pulled a large cardboard box from under the counter, and set it on the surface with a thud. One by one he lifted the devices.","\"This is the Aether 600x—fastest processor on the market right now. This one, the zPhone 5, best camera you’ll find in this price range. And this, the Cony Z6, newest model just released last week. So which one do you like?\"","The claims were clearly exaggerated. Rael examined each phone without answering. The old man watched him for a few seconds, then tilted his head.","\"Having trouble deciding, sir? Tell me what you’re really looking for.\"","\"Best security features,\" Rael said.","The owner’s eyes lit up with understanding. He bent down again, rummaged deeper, and came up with a single plain black box. He placed it on the counter almost carefully.","\"Dinux X series. Fully decentralized. No connection to the main networks unless you allow it. Built-in isolation so no outside device can see what you’re doing. Strong protection against most viruses and tracking software. For someone who wants to stay private, this is the one.\"","Rael picked up the box and turned it over in his hands. The specifications matched what he needed.","\"I’ll take it.\"","The old man looked pleased. He carried the phone to the register and printed a simple bill. Rael scanned his old phone against the payment plate. Six hundred credits transferred at once. He opened the new device, set a temporary name and number, and finished the basic configuration. Every new phone meant a new number—exactly what he wanted.","As Rael prepared to leave, the owner held up another small box.","\"I’ve got a matching smartwatch. They work well together.\"","Rael considered it for a second, then nodded. \"All right. Add it.\"","He paid the extra amount and slipped both boxes straight into his storage ring. The old man’s eyes flicked to the ring, but he said nothing. Greed outweighed curiosity.","\"We also have ovens,\" the owner offered, already reaching under the counter again.","Rael gave a short, dry smile. \"Don’t push your luck.\"","The old man chuckled, belly shaking, and raised both hands in surrender. \"Fair enough. You know where to find me if you need anything else.\"","\"Yeah. Bye.\"","Rael stepped back out into the cold street, the new phone already in his pocket and the shop bell chiming once more behind him.","---"]},{"number":40,"title":"Chapter 40: Place to stay [1]","paragraphs":["Rael stepped out of the phone shop and immediately opened a crypto app on the new device. It asked for almost nothing—just a name and a number. He typed in Rael Ashborne without hesitation. The Blackthorn name no longer belonged to him. They had thrown him out; he saw no reason to keep carrying it.","He transferred the remaining nine million credits from the old account. The process finished in under two minutes. Once the balance showed on the new wallet, he took out the old phone. Photos of friends, family, even private messages from the girl he once liked still sat in the gallery. He felt nothing looking at them. Those people had shown their real faces the moment he became useless. He was glad they had. He dropped the phone on the sidewalk, raised his heel, and smashed it once, twice, until the screen spider-webbed into useless glass. Then he kicked the pieces into a public dustbin and walked on.","His stomach growled hard enough to hurt. All he had consumed since returning was one cup of coffee. A few meters ahead a street cart glowed under a flickering red neon sign. The smell of grilled meat cut through the usual city smoke. Rael joined the short line. When his turn came he ordered a large hot dog with extra cheese.","The vendor—an older man with a stained apron and a permanent scowl—worked in silence, slapped the finished dog into a paper tray, and took the crypto payment with a grunt. Rael took the first bite while still standing there. After months of half-burned monster meat and eventually no salt at all, the simple rush of fat, cheese, and soft bread felt almost luxurious. He finished it in four more bites and kept walking.","On the new phone he opened a housing map and filtered for available rentals. Most of the decent places near the Official Dimensional Gate were marked expensive. He flagged a few options, then raised a hand. A ground taxi slid to the curb with a soft hydraulic hiss.","Rael climbed in and gave the address of the contractor’s office. The driver nodded once and pulled into traffic.","The taxi climbed onto an elevated bridge. Through the window the city stretched out in layered lights—neon signs, floating ad boards, and the dark silhouettes of towers. In the distance one building rose higher than all the others, a black monolith with the glowing AAA crest near its peak: the Awakener Authority Association. Rael had no intention of going there today. Shelter first. Everything else later.","The taxi stopped in front of a narrow three-story office wedged between a pawn shop and a closed noodle stand. Rael paid and stepped out. The same contractor who had sold him the old apartment still worked here. He had a score to settle.","Inside, the reception area was small and over-lit. A woman with tightly pulled hair sat behind a scratched metal desk. She looked up, gave a practiced smile, and asked what he needed. Rael said he wanted to rent. She checked the schedule, typed something, and told him to wait. He sat on a plastic chair that creaked under even his weight. Five minutes later she called his name—well, the name he had given—and pointed to the inner door.","Daniel sat behind a wider desk in a cheap dark suit. His black hair was slicked back with too much product, and the lights made it look greasy. He glanced up as Rael entered and offered a polite, empty smile.","\"Mr. Rael, please sit down. What can I do for you?\"","Rael sat. \"I need a place to rent.\"","Daniel tapped his tablet and began scrolling through listings, tilting the screen so Rael could see. \"Any particular area?\"","\"Near the Official Dimensional Gate.\"","Daniel’s eyebrows rose. \"Those run higher than average. You understand why.\"","\"I understand.\"","Daniel kept scrolling, making small talk. \"Where are you staying right now?\"","\"I used to live in the XYZ apartments,\" Rael said flatly. \"The owner sold the place out from under me.\"","Daniel frowned, still not recognizing him. \"That’s odd. Those units fall under my jurisdiction.\"","Rael dropped Conceal.","The change was subtle but immediate. Daniel’s eyes widened. The color drained from his face so fast it looked like someone had pulled a plug. He remembered the quiet young man who had paid cash for the apartment months ago—the Blackthorn heir the news later declared missing. He had assumed the kid was dead or gone for good and had quietly resold the unit last month for a second profit.","\"M-Mr. Rael—Blackthorn—\"","Rael said nothing. He simply watched.","Daniel shot up from his chair so fast it rolled backward and hit the wall. He hurried around the desk and dropped to one knee beside Rael’s chair, hands clasped.","\"I’m sorry. I’m so sorry. I thought— the articles said missing— I never would have—\"","Rael looked down at him with complete indifference. \"Give me my money back first.\"","Daniel scrambled back to his seat, fingers shaking as he opened the payment interface. \"Of course, of course.\" He entered a number. \"Five hundred thousand. Please, scan here.\"","Rael didn’t move. \"With interest.\"","Daniel’s throat worked. He changed the amount. \"Eight hundred thousand. That’s more than fair, yes?\"","Rael kept staring.","A single bead of sweat rolled down Daniel’s temple. His voice cracked. \"One million. One million credits. Please.\"","Only then did Rael lift his new phone and complete the transfer. The moment the confirmation appeared he gave a small, polite smile that did not reach his eyes.","\"Happy to do business with you.\"","Daniel stayed frozen in his chair, face pale, hands still trembling on the tablet. In his mind the quiet customer across the desk had transformed into something far more dangerous than any ordinary client.","Rael smoothed the front of his jacket once, and Daniel was happy like a man who had just survived a mugging by an extremely polite bully.","---","[Strome2warden thanks for the gift.] 😊"]},{"number":41,"title":"Chapter 41: Place to stay [2]","paragraphs":["Rael remained seated across from Daniel and spoke as if the earlier confrontation had never happened.","\"Now then. About the place I want to rent.\"","Daniel had clearly hoped the matter ended with the money. His smile looked strained. \"Of course. What kind of unit are you looking for? One-bedroom or two?\"","\"Two-bedroom. Preferably close to the Dimensional Gate.\"","Daniel knew better than to argue. He pulled up the listings again and turned the tablet so Rael could see. After a short review Rael selected a unit with strong security measures, only two kilometers from the gate.","\"This one.\"","Daniel began the paperwork without complaint. \"When would you like to move in?\"","\"Now. I have nowhere else to stay because of a certain someone.\"","Daniel gave an awkward little laugh and called his secretary. She entered a moment later carrying a tray with a set of keys and the printed contract. Rael briefly activated Conceal so she would not see his real face. Daniel noticed the shift watching Rael’s features change into and ordinary looking person but he kept silent. Rael signed, took the keys, and stood.","\"Have my things from the old apartment brought over.\"","Daniel’s expression said he felt like a servant, yet he only nodded. \"Of course.\"","Outside, evening had already settled over the city. Rael climbed into another taxi. Through the window he watched the sky turn soft orange and pink as the sun dropped behind the towers. For the first time in months he felt no immediate threat. Inside the Dream Realm every day had been a fight to stay alive. There had been no time to notice a sunset, no space to simply sit and breathe. The quiet of the taxi felt almost unreal.","The vehicle stopped in front of a clean thirty-story building. This was one of the better districts—quiet streets, well-kept sidewalks, the kind of place where important people preferred to live. Rael paid the driver and walked to the entrance. A guard stepped forward. Rael showed the rental documents. The tension in the man’s shoulders eased at once.","\"Please set up biometric access for future entry,\" the guard said.","Rael followed the short procedure—fingerprints and retinal scan linked to both the building and his specific unit. When it was finished the guard pointed him toward the elevators.","A woman was already waiting in front of the lift. She looked mature, mid-thirties perhaps, with calm features and black hair gathered in a neat bun. She wore a simple half-sleeve black shirt that did little to hide a full figure. Rael glanced at her only long enough to check for mana. He felt nothing. Either she was an ordinary person or someone strong enough to hide her presence completely. After everything with Aurelia he was inclined to assume the first.","She noticed his brief look and offered a warm, polite smile. Rael returned it. The elevator arrived. He pressed the button for the nineteenth floor. She pressed nothing. They rode in comfortable silence. When the doors opened she stepped out first and walked a short distance down the hall before disappearing into an apartment. Rael realized they shared the same floor.","His own unit was the door immediately beside hers. He used the key, then the biometric lock. The door opened with a soft click.","Inside, the lights came on automatically. The apartment was fully furnished—sofa, table, shelves, even a small dining set—but everything was still wrapped in clear plastic from the previous preparation. Rael peeled the covering from the sofa and sat down. The cushions were soft. The air was clean and still. After the constant noise and danger of the Dream Realm the ordinary quiet felt almost heavy. He leaned back and let his head rest against the top of the sofa.","It still felt strange to live this way again. Comfortable. Safe. He decided he would buy a househelp robot later so the place would stay that way without much effort. For now he simply sat and listened to the silence.","A knock sounded at the door. On the small security screen he saw two men standing with several boxes. He opened the door. They explained that Daniel had sent them with the belongings recovered from the previous apartment. Rael signed the tablet they held out and stepped aside. The movers carried the boxes in and set them neatly against one wall.","When the door closed again the apartment felt a little less empty. Rael returned to the sofa, the last light of evening fading beyond the windows, and allowed himself a few more minutes of uninterrupted quiet.","---"]},{"number":42,"title":"Chapter 42: Gossips","paragraphs":["Night had settled fully over the city by the time Rael finally sat down with nothing left to do. The apartment was quiet. The plastic-covered furniture still gave the place a temporary feel, but the silence itself felt like a luxury. For the first time since returning he could look at his status without interruption or danger hanging over him.","He remembered the system message that had appeared when he nearly died: System 2.0. He had never heard the term before. In the real world the Status was a simple window—stats, skills, nothing more. It was not some overpowered game system that handed out quests or cheats. Yet the words still lingered in his mind like the opening screen of a game he had just unlocked.","\"Status.\"","The familiar panel unfolded in front of him, cleaner and more detailed than before.","╔══════════ STATUS ═════════╗","Name: Rael","Race: Human","Level: 13","Main Class: Analyze (E-Rank), Infernomancer (B-Rank)","Bloodline: Abyssal Sovereign","Stage: Dormant","Effect: +100% Growth Multiplier","━━━━━━━━ ATTRIBUTES ━━━━━━━━","Strength: 42","Agility: 48","Mana: 46","Vitality: 42","Intelligence: 10 (New) (Sealed)","Unallocated Stats: 6","━━━━━━━━ RESOURCES ━━━━━━━━━","Souls: 23","HP: 42 / 42","━━━━━━━━ SKILLS ━━━━━━━━━━━","Inferno Bolt (Expert) (5%)","Hellfire (Expert) (3%)","Flame Manipulation (Master) (4%)","━━━━━━ BLOODLINE SKILLS ━━━━━━━","Conceal (Grandmaster)","╚═════════════════════════╝","A quiet thrill ran through him. It felt exactly like the moment in a game when the character finally received the long-awaited class upgrade and a handful of new skills. The numbers were higher. The options were wider. He wanted to test everything at once.","Infernomancer had replaced Flamecaller completely. Archer and Soulbinder were gone—sacrificed during the evolution. His original Copy class had become Analyze at E-Rank. He opened the description.","[Analyze] (E-Rank)","Allows you to analyze a copied class. If you fully understand the properties and principles of the copied class, you can permanently make it your Main Class.","Slots: 3 Remaining","Then his eyes landed on the line that did not belong.","Intelligence: 10 (New) (Sealed)","10. The number was absurd. Before awakening he had been the top student of his year. His memory was closer to perfect than photographic; once he saw something he almost never forgot it. Yet during the first realm Aurelia had manipulated him with almost no effort, and there had been moments when his thoughts felt strangely slow. The sealed status suggested someone—or something—had interfered. Leveling up was the only clear path to raising it. He would have to grow stronger simply to think as clearly as he once had.","Then other implications settled in slowly, then all at once. If he could fully understand a class, he could keep it forever. No more one-month limit. Combined with Conceal, the possibilities were almost unfair. He could become anyone. A perfect infiltrator. A first-rate assassin if he ever chose that path. The thought was tempting for half a second, but it was not what he wanted. His old dream had always been the same: a mage-warrior who could fight with both blade and spell. His family had drilled dark-element swordsmanship into him since childhood. If his affinity had been dark instead of fire he could have used those forms already. For now fire would have to do. He would work with what he had.","He turned his attention to the new Infernomancer skills. Inferno Bolt, Hellfire, and Flame Manipulation each carried a small percentage next to the mastery rank. The system had told him earlier that mastery could rise through use and understanding alone. No souls required. That single change made everything feel different. It was like being handed a character who could grow infinitely as long as the player kept practicing. He found himself already planning the next day’s training—how to shape the flames, how far he could push control, how the new skills would feel in real combat.","He closed the status window and picked up the new phone. The quiet excitement of the power-up still hummed under his skin, but curiosity about the world he had left behind pulled stronger. He opened the news and went straight to the trending list of new rising stars.","Faces from the academy filled the feed. Many of his former classmates had already cleared the First Realm and were making names for themselves in Aetherion, the Second Realm. Most awakeners returned within a month. The fact that people were still missing after four months in Bloomingdale remained an unsolved stain on the city’s record.","One name sat at the top of nearly every article.","Seraphine Strombane.","Daughter of the Strombane family head, another sovereign house on the same level as the Blackthorns. Rael had seen her at university and at formal gatherings. They had never been friends. She had always ranked second to him in exams and practical evaluations, and she had never hidden her dislike. He had never returned the hostility; being first had simply been normal. If someone hated him for it, the solution was to surpass him. Now the articles declared her this year’s top awakener. She had taken the position he once occupied without effort. A faint, unfamiliar sting of jealousy surfaced before he pushed it aside.","Further down he found Adrian Gale.","Lightning Swordsman—S-Rank. The same irritating presence from university, born into yet another sovereign family, impossible to ignore completely. The article showed him standing with a party that made Rael stop scrolling.","Eve Starborne and Matthew Rockwell.","His former best friends. Eve had once had a quiet crush on him. Rael never returned her feelings but still considered her a good friend; Matthew had clearly liked her all that time and later he had grown strange whenever the three of them were together. And when Rael was classified as Classless he was the one who was most happy. But what Rael find funny was according to the article, Adrian and Eve had been found together inside the Dream Realm and were now publicly linked romantically. Neither had confirmed a relationship, but the photos and captions left little doubt. Rael stared at the screen for a second, then let out a short, genuine laugh. He could almost picture Matthew’s face when the news reached him—the moment he thought the path was finally clear, only for Adrian to walk away with her. The absurdity of it felt sharper than it should have. \"Wait why am I liking gossips so much.\" For a brief moment Rael wondered whether the less Intelligence was making the gossip more entertaining than it deserved to be.","He kept reading. Other names appeared—new guild recruitments, early dungeon clears, a few quiet scandals among the noble heirs who had returned on schedule. The four months he had missed were already being written into official records and social columns. The world had continued without him, assigning new ranks and new favorites while he fought to stay alive in a place that should not have existed.","Rael set the phone down on the sofa and leaned back. The apartment remained quiet. Outside the window the city lights glowed in layered bands of neon and mana-lamp white. The power-up still sat in his chest like a banked fire, waiting. Tomorrow he would test the new skills. Tomorrow he would begin turning percentages into real control. Tonight, for the first time in a long while, he simply sat with the knowledge that he was stronger than when he had left, and that the game—whatever shape it now took—had only just started again. He is going to rise to the top once again.","---"]},{"number":43,"title":"Chapter 43: Nightmare","paragraphs":["At some point while reading the articles, Rael’s eyes closed.","When he opened them again he was sitting against a dead, blackened tree. The trunk was split and charred, the branches twisted like broken fingers. The sky above was a deep, unnatural red. No sun. No clouds. Only that solid crimson pressing down.","Blood began to rise around him.","It came slowly at first, seeping up from the cracked ground, warm and thick. It reached his ankles, then his calves. He tried to stand and discovered the ropes. Coarse, wet rope bound his wrists and chest to the tree. The blood kept climbing. It covered his knees. He shouted—something he almost never did.","\"Help! Anyone!\"","The blood rose to his waist. It was heavy, pulling at him, filling the air with the iron stench of a slaughterhouse. He pulled against the ropes until the bark bit into his skin.","\"Rael...\"","The voice came from a short distance away. He turned his head so fast his neck cracked.","Lila stood in the blood. She looked exactly as she had in the cave—pale, tired, but alive. For one second relief hit him so hard he almost laughed.","\"Lila—how are you—\"","\"Rael.\" Her voice was quiet. Then it rose, sharp and raw. \"Why didn’t you save me?\"","The words hit like a physical blow. \"No. No, it wasn’t— I tried—\"","Her eyes emptied. The color drained out of them until only hollow sockets remained. Blood spilled from the corners and ran down her cheeks in thick lines. She collapsed forward. Both legs were gone below the thighs. She began to crawl toward him through the rising blood, dragging herself with her hands, the stumps leaving dark trails behind her.","\"Because of you I died.\"","He threw himself backward against the tree, ropes cutting deeper. \"No—no, please—\"","She kept coming. The blood was up to his chest now. Her hollow face was only a few meters away. He squeezed his eyes shut and waited for the impact.","Nothing came.","He opened them again.","Aurelia sat on his lap as if she had always been there. The red sky framed her hair. She smiled the same soft, terrible smile she had worn while eating brains.","\"I love you, Rael.\"","She leaned in and kissed him. Her teeth closed on his lower lip, then kept going. Skin tore. Flesh parted. Pain exploded across his face as she bit deeper, ripping outward, blood pouring down his chin and throat.","He woke with a violent gasp.","He was sitting upright on the sofa in the new apartment. Morning light leaked through the windows. His hands flew to his mouth. Lips. Intact. Whole. He pressed his fingers against them hard enough to hurt, confirming they were still there. His heart hammered so violently he could feel it in his throat. Sweat soaked the back of his shirt.","A dream. A nightmare.","He forced slow breaths until the shaking in his hands eased. The fear that lingered felt wrong—deeper than anything he had felt even while Aurelia’s tentacles were crushing him. In the realm he had acted. Here, in the dream, he had only been helpless.","Outside the window the sun was just clearing the towers. Early morning. He stood, legs unsteady, and walked to the bathroom attached to the hall. The apartment was larger than it had seemed the night before—an open kitchen connected to the living space, two bedrooms further in. He had not even opened the bedroom doors yet.","He turned the shower on and stepped under the water. The heat helped. He stayed there a long time, letting the dream drain away with the water. When he finally stepped out he dried his hair with a towel, dressed in clean clothes from the delivered boxes, and decided the nightmare could wait. There were real things to handle today.","First, the apartment. Plastic still covered nearly every surface. He started in the kitchen, peeling wrap from the counters and cabinets, wiping down shelves that had never been used. He left the main hall for later and moved to the bedrooms. The larger one he claimed for himself. While he cleaned he thought, absently, that no sovereign child would ever do this work. They had armies of servants. He was probably the first of his kind to scrub his own floors. He was not particularly good at it, but he improved with every minute, movements becoming faster and more efficient.","Even so, the entire place would take the whole day. He needed help—or at least the right tools. He changed into outdoor clothes and decided to go buy a cleaning robot and handle the other items on his list.","As he locked the apartment door the neighbor from the previous evening appeared in the hallway, carrying a small bag of trash. She looked at him and gave a polite bow. Rael returned it.","\"I live next door,\" she said. \"I didn’t realize we had the same floor.\"","\"I only moved in yesterday.\"","\"If you need anything, tell me. Neighbors should help each other.\"","\"Thank you. I’ll remember that.\"","The exchange was ordinary, almost warm. She disappeared back into her apartment. Something about her still felt slightly off—not hostility, just a quiet wrongness he could not name. He filed it away and walked to the elevator.","Downstairs the morning traffic was already moving. Cars pulled out of the underground parking in a steady stream. He would need a vehicle of his own soon; an awakener without reliable transport was at a constant disadvantage. First, though, the store.","He ordered a taxi. The same guard from yesterday gave him a small nod of recognition. Rael nodded back. People here were almost too friendly.","The taxi ride was short. The driver, an older man with a perpetual half-smile, made a dry joke about customers buying their own cars and putting honest drivers out of work. Rael gave a short answering smile and let the rest of the journey pass in silence. He was heading to the best automobile store in the city for a reason.","The taxi slowed and stopped in front of a wide glass-fronted building. The sign above the entrance read Nexla Automobile Store in clean white letters.","---","[Strom2warden Thanks for the Gift]"]},{"number":44,"title":"Chapter 44: New Vehicle","paragraphs":["The glass doors of Nexla Automobile Store slid open with a soft hiss. Cold air-conditioning washed over Rael the moment he stepped inside, a sharp contrast to the chilly but polluted street. The showroom was vast—two full floors of polished black flooring and white light panels that made every vehicle gleam. Some cars floated a few centimeters above the ground on quiet anti-gravity fields. Others sat on traditional wheels. A row of luxury sedans occupied the far wall, their surfaces reflecting the overhead lights like liquid metal. The entire place smelled of new circuitry, treated leather, and the faint clean scent of high-grade mana stones.","A young salesman in a fitted grey uniform noticed him almost immediately and walked over with a practiced smile.","\"Looking for transportation, sir?\" he asked, gesturing toward the open floor.","\"Yes,\" Rael answered. \"Show me what you have. I’m not completely sure what I need yet.\"","\"Of course.\" The salesman launched into the catalog with easy confidence. \"We have Mana Bikes, starting with the basic models. Magitech Cars if you want something more comfortable for daily use. Hover Carriages if you prefer to travel the way nobles do. Mana Boards are cheaper and popular with younger customers for short distances and fun. We also carry standard cars powered by mana stones with a ten-year shelf life. Teleportation Bracelets exist as well—very fast, but they only work over short ranges, you have to place a rune at the destination first, and they drain a lot of stones.\"","He paused, studying Rael for a second. The salesman could clearly sense the density of mana around him and adjusted his tone.","\"If you’re looking for a companion inside the realms, we have options there too. Magitech Horses. Summoned Mount contracts—lesser dragons, wyverns, reinforced beasts. Those run higher, of course. And if money truly isn’t a concern, we keep a few Personal Teleporters in the back. Those require a government permit.\"","Rael let the list wash over him while his eyes moved across the displays. He needed something practical: fast, convenient, and low-profile. The luxury cars reminded him of the vehicles his family once used, but even the best models here fell short of what the Blackthorns kept in their private garages. He did not want anything that large or eye-catching.","His attention settled on the bike section.","Three clear categories stood side by side. Traditional wheeled motorbikes. Hover bikes that floated a hand-span above the ground. Full flying bikes designed for open air lanes. He had learned basic handling years ago, but he had never ridden a flying model. Yesterday’s near-miss with the reckless hover bike on the street still sat in his memory. Speed like that had its uses.","\"Do you have anything convertible?\" he asked. \"Hover mode that can switch to full flight.\"","The salesman’s eyes brightened. \"Yes, sir. New models released this summer. Follow me.\"","They took a quiet escalator to the upper floor. The second level was dedicated almost entirely to high-performance bikes. Different manufacturer logos glowed softly above each display—sharp, competing brands fighting for the same customers. The salesman led him to a unit near the center of the floor.","The bike was black with clean grey accents. Low profile. Aggressive lines. No unnecessary chrome. It looked like it had been designed to cut through air rather than merely travel on it.","\"This is the CoreMotion 3-1 X60,\" the salesman said. \"Three-way convertible—wheel mode, hover mode, and full flight. Fastest model currently on the open market.\"","A translucent screen floated beside the bike, listing every specification in crisp white text. Top speed in hover: four hundred kilometers per hour. Flight ceiling high enough for the secondary traffic lanes. Mana-stone efficiency rated for long cross-city runs. Adaptive suspension. Silent mode for low-profile travel. Integrated navigation and threat-detection suite.","The price sat at the bottom: one hundred thousand credits. Not cheap, but well within what Rael could spend without noticing.","He walked a slow circle around the machine. The frame looked solid. The seat was positioned for control rather than comfort. Even standing still the bike gave off a quiet sense of readiness, the way a drawn bow feels before the arrow is released. It was exactly the kind of machine that belonged in the half-formed fantasies of every boy who had ever watched hover racers scream past on the news.","\"I’ll take it.\"","The salesman smiled the genuine smile of someone who had just closed a high-value sale. \"Excellent choice. Fast, reliable, easy to maintain, and still far cheaper than most cars. CoreMotion Industries—one of our strongest sellers.\"","He signaled a young woman at a nearby desk. She began preparing the paperwork while the salesman guided Rael to a small waiting area and offered tea. The tea was better than expected—clean, slightly floral, clearly meant for customers spending serious money. Fifteen minutes later the woman returned with a folder, a sleek black key card, and a small bag of complimentary items.","Rael completed the payment. The key felt light in his hand. Among the free merchandise was an advanced helmet with a dark visor and a full heads-up display. He stored the empty boxes in his storage ring, swung one leg over the bike, and settled into the seat. The machine recognized the key and woke with a low, almost inaudible hum.","He chose wheel mode for the exit. The bike rolled smoothly across the polished floor and out through the side bay into ordinary street traffic. The first few blocks felt strange—too responsive, too light—but the controls were intuitive. Once he reached a clearer stretch of road he switched to hover.","The transition was seamless. The wheels folded upward into the frame. The bike lifted a short distance off the ground and the speed indicator on the dashboard climbed. Four hundred kilometers per hour became available with a simple twist of the throttle. Wind pressure increased, but the frame remained stable. Rael sealed the helmet. The visor lit up with a clean blue interface: current route, real-time traffic density, highlighted hazards, and a soft directional arrow pointing toward the old apartment district.","He eased into the flow of vehicles, first testing the handling in normal traffic, then opening the throttle once he reached the elevated highway that cut across the city. The bike responded instantly. Buildings blurred. The layered traffic of Bloomingdale spread out below and above him—ground cars, mid-level hover lanes, and the high metro lines further up. From this height half the city was visible in a single glance.","He was still getting used to the speed when a dark column of smoke caught his eye.","It rose in the direction he was already traveling, thickening quickly against the pale morning sky. Something was burning, and it was large enough to be seen from the highway. Rael leaned forward, opened the throttle further, and aimed the bike toward the growing plume.","---","Next is going to be a mass release"]},{"number":45,"title":"Chapter 45: Registration [1]","paragraphs":["Rael kept the throttle open, following the thickening column of smoke until the source came into view. A crowd had already formed a rough circle on the street. He slowed the bike and stopped a short distance away.","A half-burned green sign lay on the ground. The same sign that had hung above the mobile shop the day before. The entire storefront was blackened and collapsed. Firefighter drones hovered overhead, still spraying residual flames with targeted streams of water. Human firefighters moved through the wreckage, pulling the last of the survivors clear. After a few more minutes the fire died completely, leaving only a dark, smoking shell. Every appliance inside had been reduced to twisted metal and ash.","Two firefighters emerged carrying a body. Rael recognized the bald head and heavy build even through the damage. The shop owner’s face was half-burned away. A clean hole punched through the center of his chest. This was no accident. Someone had killed him and set the fire to cover it.","Murders in this part of the city were common enough. Most were local disputes that the authorities handled—or failed to handle—depending on how much money changed hands. But the timing sat wrong in Rael’s mind. He had used his old phone here yesterday to transfer the credits. This was the last place that phone had connected to anything. The list of people who might still be looking for him was long: Aurelia, his own family, rival houses and guilds that had hated him simply for outperforming their children. Any one of them could have tracked the signal.","An older woman pushed through the crowd, her face already streaked with tears. Two teenage children followed close behind her—a boy and a girl, both pale and shaking. When she saw the body she dropped to her knees beside it.","\"You can’t leave us,\" she cried, voice breaking. \"Not like this. We just moved here so you wouldn’t have to travel so far. You promised we’d be fine...\"","The boy grabbed his father’s burned sleeve. \"Papa—Papa, wake up—\"","The girl stood frozen for a second, then began sobbing openly, repeating the same word over and over. \"Papa... Papa...\"","The woman’s hands pressed against the ruined chest as if she could somehow close the hole. \"We need you. The children need you. You can’t leave us alone...\"","Rael’s chest tightened. The family had lived only a few doors away so the man could walk to work each morning. They had lost everything in a single night because someone decided the shop owner was a convenient lead. The people who did this treated human lives like obstacles. The man on the ground had known almost nothing—only that a customer had bought a phone. That had been enough to kill him.","Anger settled cold and clear in Rael’s mind. He refused to carry the blame for their cruelty. The only answer was strength. The only reliable path to that strength ran through the Dream Realm.","He pulled the helmet back on, started the bike, and left the scene behind. But his mind was still on the old man’s death he didn’t know what he could do now. He also guess who could it be who initiated this attack many answers came up but can’t be sure in one answer. Half an hour of steady riding brought him to the Awakener Authority Association.","The building dominated the skyline. Even from kilometers away it had been visible, a black monolith rising far above the surrounding towers. Up close the difference was absolute. Where the rest of Bloomingdale mixed neon, aging concrete, and patched magitech, the Association headquarters was pure advanced infrastructure. Seamless dark alloy walls reflected the sky without a single visible joint. Soft blue light ran in clean lines along the structure, marking security fields and internal power conduits. Floating platforms moved in controlled paths around the upper levels. The air itself felt different—filtered, denser with regulated mana. This place did not belong to the ordinary city. It stood apart, a fortress of authority that non-awakeners would never fully understand.","Rael stopped at Gate 7 and removed his helmet. He made only small adjustments with Conceal: hair shifted to dark brown, eyes to a matching brown, skin tone from pale to ordinary. Subtle changes were easier to maintain than a full rewrite of his face. Even someone who had known him before would see only a close resemblance, nothing definite.","A guard stepped forward. The man’s presence was heavy—clearly high-level, working the gate for reasons of his own.","\"Business?\"","\"My name is Rael Ashborne. I have come for the New awakener registration.\"","The guard nodded and then handed him a form and pointed toward a processing line beneath a wide overhang. He told Rael that’s where he could start the registration process. Rael filled the paper with the false details he had prepared, listing his class as Infernomancer and noting the skills he officially possessed. He still had not tested any of the new fire abilities in the real world. The thought stayed with him as he joined the queue.","The clerk behind the counter worked without an AI assistant, entering every line by hand the way people in this world still preferred. Rael had always found the refusal of automation strange, but it was simply how things were done. The clerk finished, stamped the form, and returned a printed page.","It was a complete roadmap of every step required to obtain an official Awakener ID. The list was long.","---"]},{"number":46,"title":"Chapter 46: Registration [2]","paragraphs":["The first item on the roadmap was simple and terrifying in equal measure: he had to place his hand on an Awakening Stone again.","Rael felt the old tension rise the moment he read the line. The last time he had touched one of those stones it had been in front of thousands of people—classmates, teachers, every major family in Solaria, including his own. The declaration of \"No class detected\" had echoed through the hall and broken something inside him that still had not fully healed. Today there would be no audience, yet the weight of that memory pressed against his ribs all the same.","The Church handled every official awakening stone. The section of the Association given over to them already felt different from the rest of the building. Black-robed figures moved quietly between pillars. Soft light filtered through high windows. A short line of hopeful late-awakeners stood in front of a wide set of doors. Most of them looked exhausted. Some left the chamber with blank faces and red eyes—another failure, another dream quietly ending. Late awakening was the last thin hope for people who had been declared ordinary the first time. Rael watched them and said nothing.","While he waited he studied a large board mounted on the wall. It listed the recognized tiers of human power:","Mortal → Awakened → Elite → Expert → Master → Grandmaster → Sovereign → Transcendent","Each tier was further divided into three internal stages. The gap between any two full tiers was described as enormous. His own status still refused to display a tier. Until the stone officially recognized him, the system treated him as incomplete.","A young man in a black priest’s gown called his name. Rael followed. The corridor began in the clean white of the Association, but with every step the color warmed. White gave way to soft red. The air grew heavier, scented with old incense and polished stone. By the time they stopped before a pair of tall double doors the architecture had changed completely. The priest opened the doors without a word and gestured for him to enter.","The hall beyond was vast.","High vaulted ceilings disappeared into shadow. Rows of dark wooden pews stretched toward a raised dais. Along the walls, narrow stained-glass windows cast fractured crimson light across the floor.","Rael was not a very religious person even though his family use to give big donation to the churches every year he just couldn’t believe the same values.","At the far end stood a statue that dominated the entire space. It was the statue of the God. It was carved from pale stone and covered in intricate patterns of gold. The figure had no ears, no mouth, no hair—only a single enormous golden eye set in the center of its face. The eye seemed to watch everything at once. A cold line traced its way down Rael’s spine.","\"Come here, my child.\"","The voice belonged to a nun standing at the base of the statue. She was fully covered in pale robes, only her face visible. Up close she looked no older than Rael himself. She took his wrist with surprising strength and guided him forward.","The Awakening Stone rested on a low pedestal directly beneath the statue’s gaze. It was shaped exactly like a human eye, the iris a swirl of cloudy crystal. The nun placed his hand flat against its surface and did not release him. Her other hand rested lightly on his forearm. Soft chanting began under her breath—words he could not understand.","\"Channel your mana,\" she said.","Rael hesitated only a second, then let a thin stream of mana flow into the stone.","Light exploded outward.","First pure white, so bright he could not look directly at it. The light shifted to yellow, then deepened into a rich, burning orange. The stone pulled at him. His eyes refused to close. Tears formed and dried almost instantly against the heat radiating from the crystal. The hall remained completely silent except for the nun’s continued chant.","A clear voice spoke from the stone itself.","\"Awakened... Infernomancer Class.\"","A translucent panel appeared in the air beside the pedestal:","[Infernomancer (B-Rank)]","The nun’s face broke into a genuine smile. \"Congratulations, child. A B-Rank Class you will become a great warrior.\"","Hearing a girl his own age call him child felt strange, but Rael barely noticed. The stone was not finished. A second surge of light erupted—brighter than the first. He felt the Analyze class being examined, measured, dragged into the open. Instinct took over. He tore his hand free.","The light flared one final time, flooding every corner of the hall in searing gold-white radiance before collapsing back into the stone. The sudden darkness left afterimages burning across his vision.","The nun stood frozen. A single tear ran down her cheek. Her hands trembled as she reached out, caught his wrist again, and pressed his palm against the soft skin of her face. She held it there as if confirming something only she could feel. She did not speak. She only looked at him with an expression that mixed recognition, grief, and something close to reverence.","Rael pulled his hand back carefully. \"I passed, right? I should go.\"","She blinked, returning to herself with visible effort. \"Yes... yes. Here.\" She pressed a thin green band into his hand. \"Speak with Brother Sam outside. He will guide you to the next stage.\"","Rael nodded once and walked toward the doors. They opened at his approach and closed heavily behind him.","Inside the hall the nun sank to her knees on the cold stone floor. Her voice, when it finally came, was broken and barely audible.","\"Ooh Great Father...\"","---"]},{"number":47,"title":"Chapter 47: Registration [3]","paragraphs":["The young priest guided Rael out of the church hall and down another corridor. The red stone and incense faded behind them, replaced once more by the clean white walls of the Association. They stopped at a wide set of automatic doors. The priest gestured for him to enter and then left without another word.","Inside, four other people already sat on a row of plain metal chairs. All of them looked up when Rael stepped through the doorway. The room was rectangular and windowless, lit by bright overhead panels. Several cameras hung from the ceiling, their lenses slowly panning across the group. Someone was clearly watching.","Rael took an empty seat. A moment later the large screen mounted on the far wall flickered to life. The image of a young woman with bright orange hair appeared. She was strikingly beautiful—clear skin, sharp eyes, the kind of face that held attention without effort. She opened her mouth to speak, then turned her head sharply to the left as if someone off-camera had interrupted her.","\"Wait, why do I have to do this?\"","A muffled voice answered from somewhere beside her. \"Just do it, idiot.\"","The five candidates exchanged confused glances. The orange-haired woman scowled, then forced herself to face the camera again.","\"Fine. Argh... All right. Welcome, young awakeners.\" Her tone made it obvious she would rather be anywhere else. \"For this stage you will destroy the dummies on your left. We need to measure both your aim and the raw power of your skills. This room is reserved for mage-class individuals only, so temporary wands have been provided. If you miss the target three times, you fail. You may reapply after three months.\"","The left wall slid upward with a mechanical hum, revealing a long testing range. Several white humanoid dummies stood at varying distances, their surfaces smooth and unmarked.","Tension settled over the room. Rael felt it too. In the Dream Realm he had used Fireball and Ignite at close range, often guided by the precision of the temporary Archer skills. Those skills were gone now. He would have to rely entirely on what remained.","\"Lucas Grey,\" the woman on the screen called.","A short boy with nervous eyes stood up. His hands were already trembling as he walked to the marked line and picked up one of the provided wands. The tip shook visibly.","\"Wind Cutter.\"","A thin blade of compressed air shot forward, but it lost coherence long before reaching the nearest dummy and dissipated harmlessly. Lucas’s face tightened. He tried again.","\"Wind Cutter!\"","The second attempt flew even wider, striking the reinforced ceiling with a dull thud. The room was built to handle far worse; the impact left no damage.","\"Last chance, Lucas,\" the orange-haired woman said, not unkindly but without warmth.","The boy looked ready to cry. His shoulders were locked tight, his breathing short and uneven. Rael stood and walked over. He placed a steady hand on the boy’s shoulder.","\"Lucas, right?\"","The boy nodded quickly.","\"Mind if I give you a little advice?\"","Another nod, this one filled with desperate relief.","\"First, relax your shoulders. You’re not holding a gun. The wand doesn’t need that much tension.\" Lucas forced his shoulders down. \"Now, instead of thrusting straight forward, swing the wand from left to right as you release the skill. Create a wider spiral of wind. That will let it travel farther and give you a better chance of connecting. Aim at the center of the dummy and let the motion do the work. Good luck.\"","From the chairs a girl with red hair let out a short, mocking laugh. \"If you can’t even manage this much, maybe you should just go home, crybaby.\"","Rael ignored her. Lucas drew a slow breath, adjusted his stance the way he had been told, and raised the wand again.","\"Wind Cutter.\"","This time the air twisted into a broad spiral. It crossed the distance and struck the dummy cleanly on the upper arm. White material flaked away, then immediately began to regenerate.","The woman on the screen smiled for the first time. \"Passed. You may proceed to the next stage.\"","Lucas turned to Rael, eyes bright with leftover adrenaline and gratitude. He bowed deeply. \"Thank you so much.\"","\"It wasn’t much,\" Rael said. \"You would have figured it out. I only helped with the nerves.\"","The boy bowed once more and hurried toward the exit door that had just unlocked. The orange-haired woman watched the exchange with open interest.","The red-haired girl stood and walked over to Rael, arms crossed. \"You know this is a competition, right? You can’t keep helping him. He has to learn to do it himself.\"","Rael met her gaze calmly. \"You’re not wrong. He can’t depend on someone else forever. But just now the problem wasn’t his skill—it was anxiety. If he failed today because of that, the same fear would follow him next time. I only removed the fear. Everything else he did on his own.\"","She studied him for a second, then gave a small, reluctant shrug. \"You’re weird.\"","Rael only smiled.","\"Next,\" the woman on the screen announced, \"Clara Rose.\"","The red-haired girl—Clara—walked to the line, picked up a wand, and settled into a clean stance.","\"I’m ready. Fire Slash.\"","A sharp arc of flame left the wand and struck the dummy square in the chest on the first attempt. The material blackened and peeled away before regenerating. The entire sequence took less than three seconds.","\"Passed,\" the woman said.","Clara walked toward the exit, then stopped and leaned against the wall, clearly intending to watch the remaining tests.","\"Now next—Rael Ashborne.\"","Rael stepped forward to the line and picked up the waiting wand. The cameras turned to follow him. The orange-haired woman on the screen rested her chin on one hand, suddenly paying closer attention.","He stood in the quiet room, five pairs of eyes on him, and prepared to show them what an Infernomancer could actually do.","---"]},{"number":48,"title":"Chapter 48: Registration [4]","paragraphs":["Rael stepped up to the marked line with the temporary wand in hand. He felt none of the anxiety that had gripped the others. The calm surprised even him. He closed his eyes for a brief moment, opened them again, and spoke the skill name clearly.","\"Inferno Bolt.\"","Fire bloomed into existence just above his head. It started as a small sphere of ordinary orange flame, then rapidly intensified. The temperature in the testing room climbed in seconds. Heat pressed against every surface. The white walls seemed to shimmer. On the large screen the orange-haired woman’s brows drew together as she leaned closer to her camera.","The flame compressed. It shrank toward the tip of the wand, growing smaller and denser while the heat continued to rise. What had been a visible fireball became a tight, almost white-hot core no larger than a closed fist. Then it released.","A bolt of blazing infernal fire screamed down the range. It left a glowing trail of distorted air in its wake, struck the nearest high-class dummy dead center, and detonated on impact. The dummy did not simply crack or lose a limb—it exploded outward in every direction. Fragments of reinforced material scattered across the floor and walls before the regeneration function even had time to activate. What remained was a blackened crater and drifting ash.","Silence filled the room.","Every pair of eyes stared at the destruction. The other remaining candidates sat frozen. Clara Rose, who had stayed specifically to watch, looked as if someone had struck her. Her earlier confidence was gone, replaced by pure disbelief. On the screen the orange-haired woman’s lips parted; for several seconds she forgot she was supposed to speak.","An Expert-rank mastery skill. In a basic registration test. Used by someone who had only just been confirmed as Awakened. None of them had expected anything close to this level of power.","Rael glanced down at the wand. Even without the Archer class he had once copied, something of the understanding remained. The way he had focused the bolt, the instinctive sense of trajectory and penetration—those lessons had not vanished when the class was sacrificed. Knowledge gained through a copied class, it seemed, left traces that stayed.","He turned back toward the screen. Everyone was still staring at him.","\"I passed, right?\"","The woman on the screen jerked as if waking from a trance. She nodded rapidly, almost frantically. \"Yes. Yes—passed. Proceed to the next stage.\"","Rael walked toward the exit. Clara pushed herself off the wall and followed without a word, still looking dazed.","In the control room above, chaos had erupted.","More than a hundred Association staff and analysts sat in tiered rows facing a wall of monitors. The moment the dummy exploded, every conversation stopped, then restarted at once in urgent whispers.","\"Did you see the compression ratio?\"","\"That heat signature isn’t possible for a new Awakened—\"","\"Replay it. Slow it down. The penetration happened before the explosion.\"","\"Expert-tier output at minimum. Maybe higher.\"","\"A registration candidate just erased a reinforced dummy with one skill.\"","Senior observers leaned forward, fingers flying across their consoles as they pulled raw data. The dummies used in this stage were designed to withstand repeated Intermediate-rank attacks. Destroying one outright in a single hit normally required an Expert-tier awakener with refined control. The boy on the screen had done it casually, as if testing a new tool.","On the main observation platform Susan—the orange-haired woman—turned to the woman seated beside her. Samantha sat with perfect posture, ponytail neat, expression carefully neutral. She was the same woman Rael had woken up beside two days earlier.","\"Sister,\" Susan said, \"is this normal for the outskirts?\"","Samantha shook her head slowly. \"No. I’ve never seen anything like it. Not even in the capital.\"","Susan studied her for a moment. \"Why did you push this shift onto me? Father gave you the investigator role, not me.\"","Color rose in Samantha’s cheeks. She had been ready to handle the on-screen duties herself until the final candidate of the previous group walked in. Even with the subtle changes of Conceal, she had recognized him instantly. The memory of that morning—the awkwardness, the missing clothes, the assumptions she had made—had hit her so hard she could not stay on camera.","She stood quickly, practically guiding Susan into the primary seat. \"Just this once. Please. I’ll explain later.\"","Susan watched her with narrowed eyes. She had known Samantha since childhood; the other woman had never acted this flustered. Something was off. Still, she nodded.","\"Fine. But you owe me the full story.\"","Samantha’s face smoothed back into seriousness. The earlier embarrassment vanished. \"Do you think he could be the one?\"","Susan glanced at the replay still looping on a side monitor—the bolt forming, compressing, erasing the dummy. \"Maybe. Maybe not. We’ll see.\"","Rael stepped into the waiting area for the final stage. The room was larger and quieter, with simple benches along the walls. Lucas spotted him at once and broke into a wide smile.","\"I knew you’d pass,\" the shorter boy said, walking over with an outstretched hand. Rael shook it. \"Can I know your name?\"","\"Rael Ashborne. You’re Lucas, right?\"","\"Yes. Thank you again for earlier.\"","\"No problem.\"","Clara entered a few steps behind Rael. She stopped in front of him, still searching for words.","\"What kind of monster are you?\"","Rael blinked. \"What did I do?\"","Clara opened her mouth, closed it, then opened it again. Nothing coherent came out. In the end she simply shook her head, gave up, and sat down heavily on the nearest bench. She had decided further conversation was pointless.","Rael and Lucas took seats a short distance away and spoke quietly while the last candidates filtered in. One boy never appeared; the door to the testing range finally sealed, confirming he had failed the three-miss limit.","A few minutes later Susan’s image appeared on the waiting-room screen. Her earlier irritation was gone, replaced by professional calm.","\"Congratulations on completing the first two stages,\" she said. \"We will now begin the final stage.\"","The door at the far end of the room unlocked with a soft click.","---"]},{"number":49,"title":"Chapter 49: Registration [5]","paragraphs":["Susan still looked mildly displeased on the screen, the same reluctant expression she had worn since the beginning. Rael noticed, not for the first time, how many striking women he had encountered since returning. Samantha, the neighbor women with a bun, and now this orange-haired Association official. He wondered whether he had simply never paid attention before his Intelligence was sealed, or whether something about Titan itself had shifted. He pushed the thought aside.","\"The third stage is the final one,\" Susan said. \"You may take it whenever you feel ready. There is no time limit.\"","She paused, giving the words time to settle. No one stood up to leave.","\"Very well. The third test is to fight and kill a beast brought from the Dream Realm.\"","Understanding passed through the group at once. The earlier offer to delay suddenly made perfect sense. The screen split; Susan’s image shrank to a corner while a live feed filled the rest. A swordsman was already fighting an orc inside a contained arena. Susan gestured.","\"You can watch through the window on your left if you prefer.\"","The left wall turned transparent. Everyone rose and moved closer. Below them a broad, scarred orc swung a massive arm at a lightly armored swordsman. The man leapt high, trying for an overhead strike. The orc had been waiting for exactly that. Its open hand caught him in mid-air and hurled him across the arena. He slammed into the far wall and slid down. Red warning lights flared. The collar around the orc’s neck discharged a sharp current, freezing the creature in place. Handlers rushed in, lifted the unconscious swordsman, and carried him out. The orc retreated into its cage under the continuing threat of the collar.","Rael felt a brief flicker of pity for the beast, then let it go. This was simply how the world worked.","\"Mages,\" Susan said, \"this stage is now yours. Who wants to go first?\"","Clara stepped forward before Rael could speak. \"I will.\"","\"I’ll go second,\" Rael said.","Lucas, still pale, raised a hesitant hand. \"Third...\"","The fourth candidate shook his head and requested a later attempt. Susan nodded, and a side door opened to let him leave.","A new door appeared beside the observation window. \"Clara Rose, you may begin.\"","Clara glanced at Rael. \"I’m winning this round.\"","\"All right,\" he answered, unsure what competition she thought they were in.","She opened the door and descended the short stairway into the arena.","The orc that waited for her stood nearly fifteen feet tall. Its skin was a deep, mottled green, thick as old leather and crossed with pale scars. Yellow eyes fixed on the small human who had entered its space. Heavy chains on its wrists and ankles had been released; only the shock collar remained as a final safeguard. The handlers had already withdrawn behind reinforced barriers.","Clara raised her wand. The air around her warmed.","\"Fire Blast.\"","A concentrated sphere of flame formed at the tip of the wand and launched with almost no delay. It struck the orc square in the chest and burst. Fire washed across the thick hide, bright and loud. When the flames cleared, the only result was a darkened patch of skin and a low, irritated growl. The orc barely rocked backward.","Clara’s eyes narrowed. She adjusted her stance and tried again.","\"Fire Blast.\"","The second bolt hit the same area. This time the orc turned its shoulder into the impact, letting the explosion roll off. It answered with a sudden charge. The ground shook under its weight. Clara threw herself sideways; the massive fist slammed into the sand where she had stood, sending up a spray of grit. She rolled, came up on one knee, and cast while moving.","\"Flamestrike.\"","A wide circle of fire erupted beneath the orc’s feet. Flames climbed its legs and lower torso. The creature bellowed, more in anger than pain, and stamped hard enough to scatter the burning sand. It came at her again, swinging in wide, crushing arcs. Clara kept her distance, using short bursts of speed and the reach of her magic. Every time she landed a Fire Blast the orc absorbed it with little visible damage. The hide was simply too thick, the body too massive for single-target bursts to matter quickly.","Sweat already beaded on her forehead. The arena felt hotter than it should, both from her own spells and the orc’s growing rage. She changed tactics.","Instead of aiming for the chest she targeted the joints. A Fire Blast took the orc in the left knee. The creature stumbled for half a step. She followed immediately with a Flamestrike centered on the same leg. Fire wrapped the joint and the thick muscle above it. The orc roared and lashed out with the injured limb, forcing her to leap back again. The claws on its foot passed close enough that she felt the wind of the swing.","It was learning. The next charge came in a lower, more controlled rush. Clara tried to create space with another Flamestrike, but the orc simply powered through the edge of the flames, hide smoking, and closed the distance. She barely managed to interpose a hasty Fire Blast between them. The point-blank explosion staggered the creature for a second—long enough for her to slip past its reaching hand and put ten meters of sand between them once more.","Her breathing had grown sharp. Casting while constantly moving drained mana faster than standing still. She could feel the first real edge of fatigue. The orc showed no such weakness. It lowered its shoulders and came on again, using its reach to keep her from finding a clean angle.","Clara stopped retreating.","She planted her feet, raised the wand, and began chaining the spells with deliberate precision. Fire Blast to the right knee. Flamestrike overlapping the same spot. Fire Blast to the left knee before the first had fully faded. The orc’s lower legs became a continuous mass of flame and blackening flesh. It tried to advance and nearly fell when the damaged joints refused to take its full weight cleanly. For the first time the creature looked uncertain.","She pressed the advantage. Another Flamestrike bloomed directly under its stance, forcing it to shift. The moment it planted the injured right leg to catch its balance she drove a Fire Blast into the side of that knee with everything she had left in the instant cast. Something inside the joint gave with an audible crack. The orc dropped to one knee, roaring.","Clara did not give it time to recover. She closed the distance she had spent the entire fight maintaining, raised the wand high, and poured mana into one final, concentrated Fire Blast aimed at the exposed neck. The bolt left the wand white-hot. It struck the softer flesh beneath the jaw and detonated.","Fire erupted around the orc’s head and throat. The creature’s roar turned into a choked, wet sound. It clawed at the flames, staggered, and then toppled sideways. The impact shook the arena floor. The shock collar remained quiet; the handlers did not intervene. The orc’s chest rose once, twice, then stilled.","Clara stood over it, wand still raised, shoulders rising and falling with heavy breaths. Scorch marks covered her sleeves. A thin cut on her cheek from flying sand bled slowly. She had won, but the fight had taken far more from her than the clean first-try success of the dummy stage.","Above the arena, Susan’s voice came through the speakers, professional once more.","\"Clara Rose—passed.\"","Clara lowered the wand and looked up toward the observation window where Rael and the others still watched. Her expression was no longer playful. It was tired, sharp, and quietly satisfied.","---"]},{"number":50,"title":"Chapter 50: Registration [6]","paragraphs":["Medics were already helping Clara out of the arena when Rael looked over. She was limping slightly, one cheek marked by a thin cut, her earlier confidence replaced by exhaustion. He understood now what she had meant by \"winning this round.\" She had wanted to prove she was the better fire mage. The thought struck him as strangely childish. He almost laughed.","Susan’s voice came through the speakers. \"Rael Ashborne. Your turn.\"","There was a clear undercurrent of curiosity in her tone, mixed with something sharper—anticipation, maybe, or the hope of watching overconfidence collapse.","Rael walked to the door without picking up a wand.","Susan’s eyebrows rose on the screen. \"You’re not taking the wand?\"","\"I’m fine without it.\"","Interest flickered across her face. Lucas offered a quiet \"Good luck\" as Rael passed. Rael smiled and returned the wish.","He descended into the arena. Handlers were still dragging the previous orc’s corpse toward a side gate. While he waited, a random thought surfaced: he had skipped both breakfast and lunch. His stomach felt hollow, and for some reason he was craving something sweet. He pushed the distraction aside when the opposite gate began to open.","Heavy footsteps approached. The creature that entered was noticeably larger than the one Clara had fought. Thick green hide, heavy tusks, yellow eyes already fixed on him. A shock collar sat around its neck, but the chains had been removed.","[Monster Detected]","Species: Elder Orc","Level: 13","Tier: Savage","A Savage-tier beast for a registration test. They clearly wanted to measure his full strength.","Rael raised one hand and pointed two fingers at the orc.","[Flame Manipulation]","Fire gathered at his fingertips, then stretched and hardened into the shape of an arrow. A second arrow formed beside the first. He flicked his thumb downward. Both projectiles shot forward at the same instant, striking the orc cleanly through each knee. The arrows did not simply burn the surface—they punched neat, glowing holes straight through muscle and joint. The orc staggered, legs suddenly unreliable, and let out a confused roar as flames continued to lick at the wounds from the inside.","Rael did not give it time to adapt.","[Hellfire]","Dark-red fire erupted from the sand beneath the orc’s feet and climbed in a single continuous wave. It wrapped the creature’s legs, torso, arms, and finally its head in a sheath of flame that refused to go out. The orc’s screams turned high and panicked. It thrashed, trying to beat the fire away, but every movement only fed the blaze. The heat inside the arena spiked. Within fifteen seconds the thrashing slowed. Within twenty the screams stopped. By the time the flames finally died, nothing remained but a blackened, smoking husk collapsed on the sand.","Total time from first arrow to silence: forty-eight seconds.","Rael lowered his hand and looked up at the observation window.","The control room above had gone completely still for half a second, then erupted into overlapping voices. Analysts replayed the footage, zooming in on the compression of the initial arrows and the unnatural persistence of the Hellfire. A new registration record had just been set, and it had been done without a focus tool by a candidate who had only received his class confirmation hours earlier.","On the main screen Susan stared in open disbelief. She had fully expected the missing wand to cost him. She had been looking forward to watching arrogance meet reality. Instead the beast had been reduced to charcoal before she could even settle in to enjoy the struggle. Beside her, Samantha sat equally speechless.","Rael’s voice carried clearly upward. \"I passed, right?\"","Susan’s eye twitched. In her mind a string of unflattering words formed Ofcourse you passed bastard you just burnt the beast to crispe. Aloud she only said, voice tight, \"Yes. You may exit. Your identification will be prepared shortly.\"","Rael gave Lucas a thumbs-up through the window—part encouragement, part silent message that the test was manageable—then walked back up the stairs and out of the arena wing.","The main halls of the Association were busy with people moving between departments. His stomach reminded him again that he had eaten nothing substantial all day. He stopped a passing staff member and asked for the nearest place to buy food. The man pointed him down a side corridor.","The sweet shop was small but well-stocked, tucked between a supply office and a rest area. Glass cases displayed neat rows of pastries, cakes, sandwiches, and other snacks under soft light. The air smelled of sugar, butter, and dark chocolate. Rael took his time looking over the options before settling on an overloaded chocolate pastry—thick layers of sponge, heavy ganache, and a glossy icing that looked almost excessive. He was about to pay when an Association worker in a crisp uniform approached.","\"Mr. Rael Ashborne. Your identification card is ready. Please come with me.\"","Rael glanced at the pastry still sitting on the counter. \"Could you pack this? And one more in a separate box.\"","The shopkeeper moved quickly. Rael paid, took both boxes, and followed the worker. They walked in silence to a bank of lifts. The worker pressed the button for the top floor. Rael felt a mild unease; the higher the floor, the more important the office. An ordinary ID card should not require a trip this far up. He kept the thought to himself.","The lift opened onto a quieter corridor. The worker led him to a heavy door and gestured for him to enter alone.","Rael stepped inside. The temperature dropped at once—not the sharp cold of ice, but a deeper, emptier chill, like the air had been taken from somewhere far from any sun. Behind a wide desk sat a woman he recognized immediately.","Samantha.","---"]}]);
